      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-ip-forward-map-and-solution-classes",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Inverse-problem models and solution classes",
          "deps": [
            "def-metric-space"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "scraped": [],
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Christian Clason, Regularization of Inverse Problems, Chapter 3, printed pp. 15–27",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.00617v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Tristan van Leeuwen and Christoph Brune, 10 Lectures on Inverse Problems and Imaging, Lecture 1 §§1.2–1.3",
                "url": "https://tristanvanleeuwen.github.io/IP_and_Im_Lectures/what_is.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $(X,d_X)$ and $(Y,d_Y)$ be metric spaces, called the **parameter space** and the **data space**. Let $U\\subseteq X$ be a nonempty set of **admissible truths**, and let $F:U\\to Y$ be a **forward map**. For $x\\in U$, the datum $F(x)$ is its exact observation. For $y\\in Y$, define the exact solution set\n\n$$S_F(y):=\\{x\\in U:F(x)=y\\}.$$\n\nThe datum $y$ is exactly solvable on $U$ when $S_F(y)\\ne\\varnothing$.\n\nLet $\\sim$ be an equivalence relation on $U$, meaning it is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive; truths equivalent under $\\sim$ represent the same recovery target. A **metric solution-class space** for $(U,\\sim)$ consists of a metric space $(Q,d_Q)$ and a function $q:U\\to Q$ whose image is all of $Q$ and such that $q(x)=q(x')$ if and only if $x\\sim x'$. The loss between two target classes is their metric distance $d_Q$. Exact recovery of the truth itself is the special case $Q=U$, $q(x)=x$, and $d_Q=d_X$ restricted to $U$."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-ip-identifiability-and-stability-modulus",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Identifiability and stability moduli",
          "deps": [
            "def-ip-forward-map-and-solution-classes",
            "def-metric-space",
            "lem-metric-nonnegativity"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "scraped": [],
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Christian Clason, Regularization of Inverse Problems, Chapter 3 §§3.1–3.2 and Chapter 4 §4.1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.00617v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Tristan van Leeuwen and Christoph Brune, 10 Lectures on Inverse Problems and Imaging, Lecture 1 §§1.2–1.3",
                "url": "https://tristanvanleeuwen.github.io/IP_and_Im_Lectures/what_is.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Use metric spaces $(X,d_X)$ and $(Y,d_Y)$, a nonempty admissible truth class $U\\subseteq X$, a forward map $F:U\\to Y$, and a metric solution-class space $(Q,d_Q)$ with class map $q:U\\to Q$ as follows: $q$ has image all of $Q$, and $q(x)=q(x')$ exactly when $x$ and $x'$ are equivalent for recovery. The library's metric axioms imply nonnegative metric values.\n\nThe forward map $F$ is **identifiable modulo the recovery equivalence on $U$** if\n\n$$x,x'\\in U\\text{ and }F(x)=F(x')\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q(x)=q(x').$$\n\nIt is nonidentifiable modulo that equivalence if some two admissible truths have equal data and distinct target classes.\n\nA **deterministic data-only estimator** is a function $\\widehat q:Y\\to Q$. It is exact on $U$ if $\\widehat q(F(x))=q(x)$ for every $x\\in U$. The diagram below commutes exactly under this condition; it states the defining equality of functions and is not a proof device.\n\n```tikzcd\nU \\arrow[r,\"F\"] \\arrow[dr,\"q\"'] & Y \\arrow[d,\"\\widehat q\"] \\\\\n& Q\n```\n\nA **uniform stability modulus on $U$** is a nondecreasing function $\\omega:[0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)$, with $\\omega(0)=0$ and $\\lim_{t\\downarrow0}\\omega(t)=0$, such that\n\n$$d_Q(q(x),q(x'))\\le\\omega\\bigl(d_Y(F(x),F(x'))\\bigr)\\qquad(x,x'\\in U).$$\n\nIdentifiability concerns equality of exact data; the modulus is a separate quantitative bound for all truth pairs. The estimator definition specifies no regularization procedure or convergence guarantee."
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-ip-kernel-directions-preclude-unrestricted-recovery",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Kernel directions preclude unrestricted recovery",
          "deps": [
            "def-ip-forward-map-and-solution-classes",
            "def-ip-identifiability-and-stability-modulus",
            "def-metric-space",
            "lem-metric-nonnegativity"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "justified_by": [],
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "scraped": [],
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Christian Clason, Regularization of Inverse Problems, Chapter 3 and Chapter 4 §4.1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.00617v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Tristan van Leeuwen and Christoph Brune, 10 Lectures on Inverse Problems and Imaging, Lecture 1 §§1.2–1.3",
                "url": "https://tristanvanleeuwen.github.io/IP_and_Im_Lectures/what_is.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $(X,d_X)$, $(Y,d_Y)$, and $(Q,d_Q)$ be metric spaces, let $U\\subseteq X$ be a nonempty admissible truth class, let $F:U\\to Y$ be a forward map, and let $q:U\\to Q$ assign each truth its target class. Let $x_0,x_1\\in U$, let $\\delta\\ge0$, and suppose a single datum $y\\in Y$ satisfies\n\n$$d_Y(y,F(x_0))\\le\\delta\\quad\\text{and}\\quad d_Y(y,F(x_1))\\le\\delta.$$\n\nFor every deterministic data-only estimator, meaning every function $\\widehat q:Y\\to Q$, the two target-class losses at that datum satisfy\n\n$$\\max\\{d_Q(\\widehat q(y),q(x_0)),d_Q(\\widehat q(y),q(x_1))\\}\\ge\\frac12d_Q(q(x_0),q(x_1)).$$\n\nIn particular, if $F(x_0)=F(x_1)$ but $q(x_0)\\ne q(x_1)$, no data-only estimator exactly recovers both truths. The quantitative bound also holds whenever the radius-$\\delta$ data balls around the two forward data share the observed datum $y$."
        }
      ]
    }

{
      "id": "inverse-problems-identifiability-and-stability-examples",
      "title": "Inverse maps, identifiability, and stability — Examples and Counterexamples",
      "kind": "B",
