      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-ip-01-scope-and-boundary-cases",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "Kernel ambiguity, an unstable diagonal inverse, and a constrained class",
          "deps": [
            "def-ip-forward-map-and-solution-classes",
            "def-ip-identifiability-and-stability-modulus",
            "thm-ip-kernel-directions-preclude-unrestricted-recovery",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "def-linear-map",
            "lem-metrics-on-rn",
            "def-square-summable-family-on-an-arbitrary-index-set",
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "lem-square-summable-sequences-form-a-hilbert-space",
            "lem-geometric-sequence-null",
            "lem-power-monotone"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "generation": {
            "role": "example"
          },
          "sources": {
            "scraped": [],
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Christian Clason, Regularization of Inverse Problems, Chapter 3 §§3.1–3.2 and Chapter 4 §4.1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.00617v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Tristan van Leeuwen and Christoph Brune, 10 Lectures on Inverse Problems and Imaging, Lecture 1 §§1.2–1.3",
                "url": "https://tristanvanleeuwen.github.io/IP_and_Im_Lectures/what_is.html"
              },
              {
                "title": "Giannis Daras et al., A Survey on Diffusion Models for Inverse Problems, §1.2 (scope context)",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.00083v1"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "For $n=1,2$, give $\\mathbb R^n$ the metric $d_1(x,z)=\\sum_{k<n}|x_k-z_k|$, using the coordinatewise real vector-space structure. In the first model take parameter space and admissible truth class $X=U=\\mathbb R^2$, data space $Y=\\mathbb R^1$, and identity recovery target $Q=U$ with $q=\\operatorname{id}_U$ and the restricted $d_1$ metric. Define $A:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^1$ by $A(a,b)=(a)$, take $F:=A$, and define $\\ker A:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:A(x)=(0)\\}$. Then $\\ker A=\\{(0,t):t\\in\\mathbb R\\}$ is nontrivial. The truths $x_0=(0,0)$ and $x_1=(0,1)$ have the same exact datum $(0)$ and target-class distance $d_1(x_0,x_1)=1$, so every deterministic data-only estimator at that datum has worst-case loss at least $1/2$.\n\nLet $\\mathbb N=\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$ and use the real Hilbert space $\\ell^2(\\mathbb N,\\mathbb R)$. For each $n\\in\\mathbb N$, let $e_n$ be the sequence with value $1$ at $n$ and $0$ elsewhere. Define the diagonal map $D:\\ell^2\\to\\ell^2$ by $(Dx)_n=(1/2)^n x_n$. In the second model take $X=U=Y=Q=\\ell^2(\\mathbb N,\\mathbb R)$ with its canonical norm metric $d_2(x,z)=\\|x-z\\|_2$, forward map $F:=D$, and identity target map $q=\\operatorname{id}_U$. This map is well-defined, linear, and injective, hence identifiable, but it has no uniform stability modulus on all of $\\ell^2$: $\\|e_n\\|_2=1$ and $\\|De_n\\|_2=(1/2)^n\\to0$. For each $n$, the observed datum $y=0$ lies within noise radius $\\delta=(1/2)^n$ of both $D(0)$ and $D(e_n)$, so every deterministic data-only estimator at $y=0$ has worst-case loss at least $1/2$.\n\nFinally, restrict $A$ to $U_0:=\\{(a,0):a\\in\\mathbb R\\}$ and use $U_0$ itself as the target class with its inherited $d_1$ metric. The restricted map is identifiable, and its inverse $\\widehat q((a))=(a,0)$ is an isometry; the function $\\omega(t)=t$ is a uniform stability modulus. This restriction removes the particular kernel ambiguity above."
        }
      ]
    }

{
      "id": "linear-operators-pseudoinverses-and-picard-criteria",
      "title": "Compact linear inverse maps and the Picard criterion",
      "kind": "A",
      "category": "inverse-problems",
      "label": "IP-02-A",
      "companion": "linear-operators-pseudoinverses-and-picard-criteria-examples",
