            ]
          },
          "statement": "With standard Euclidean inner products, take $K:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^2$ given\nby $Kx=(x,0)$. Define $K^*:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ by $K^*(a,b)=a$; for\nall $x\\in\\mathbb R$ and $z\\in\\mathbb R^2$ it satisfies\n$\\langle Kx,z\\rangle=\\langle x,K^*z\\rangle$, the defining adjoint identity.\nFor this map let $R(K):=\\{Kx:x\\in\\mathbb R\\}$ and\n$N(K^*):=\\{z\\in\\mathbb R^2:K^*z=0\\}$. Its sole positive singular pair is\n$s_1=1$, $v_1=1$, $u_1=(1,0)$: these are unit vectors spanning the input and\nrange, and $Kv_1=s_1u_1$, $K^*u_1=s_1v_1$. Define the Picard sum for this\npair by\n$$P_K(y):=\\frac{|\\langle y,u_1\\rangle|^2}{s_1^2}.$$\nThen $y=(0,1)$ satisfies $P_K(y)=0<\\infty$ and\n$y\\in N(K^*)\\setminus\\{0\\}$, but $y\\notin R(K)$."
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-ip-dense-nonclosed-diagonal-range",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "A compact diagonal map with dense nonclosed range",
          "deps": [
            "cor-archimedean-reciprocal",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-hilbert-space-adjoint",
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "def-ip-compact-linear-inverse-model",
            "def-natural-numbers",
            "def-square-summable-family-on-an-arbitrary-index-set",
            "lem-finite-rank-operators-are-compact",
            "lem-square-summable-sequences-form-a-hilbert-space",
            "thm-ip-picard-range-criterion-for-compact-operators",
            "thm-norm-limit-of-compact-operators-is-compact",
            "thm-p-series-real-exponents"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Christian Clason, Regularization of Inverse Problems: Lecture Notes, arXiv:2001.00617v2, Theorems 3.9–3.10, §§3.1–3.2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.00617"
              },
              {
                "title": "Vesa Kaarnioja, Inverse Problems: Fourth Lecture, FU Berlin, 8 May 2023, PDF pp. 10–12",
                "url": "https://vkaarnioja.github.io/ip23/week4.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. On the real Hilbert space\n$H=\\ell^2(\\mathbb N,\\mathbb R)$, define the diagonal map\n$(Kx)_n=x_n/(n+1)$ for $n\\in\\mathbb N$, where $\\mathbb N=\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$\nand $e_n$ is the coordinate vector at $n$. In positive indexing, its singular\nsystem is $s_j=1/j$ and $v_j=u_j=e_{j-1}$ for $j\\ge1$. Then $K$ is compact and\nits range is dense but not closed. The datum $y=(1/(n+1))_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$\nlies in $H$ and in $\\overline{R(K)}$, but not in $R(K)$; its Picard sum\n$$\\sum_{j=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{|\\langle y,e_{j-1}\\rangle|^2}{s_j^2}$$\ndiverges, so the equation $Kx=y$ has no exact solution."
        }
      ]
