        {
          "id": "def-ip-isonormal-white-noise-experiment",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Isonormal Gaussian white-noise inverse experiment",
          "statement": "Let $H$ be a separable real Hilbert space, where separable means having a countable dense subset, and let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space. A real isonormal Gaussian process on $H$ is a family $Z(g)$ of real random variables with finite second moments, indexed by $g\\in H$, such that $g\\mapsto[Z(g)]$ is linear into $L^2(\\mathbb P)$, $\\mathbb E Z(g)=0$, and $\\mathbb E[Z(g)Z(h)]=\\langle g,h\\rangle_H$. It is also required that for every finite orthonormal tuple $(e_1,\\ldots,e_m)$ the variables $Z(e_1),\\ldots,Z(e_m)$ are independent and each has density $\\varphi(t)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}e^{-t^2/2}$. For any finite list of vectors, expressing them in an orthonormal basis of their finite-dimensional span shows that their process values have the corresponding linear-image Gaussian law. Here $L^2(\\mathbb P)$ identifies square-integrable random variables that agree almost surely. When $H\\ne\\{0\\}$, all unit-vector marginals have the same law; write $N(a,\\sigma^2)$ for the law of $a+\\sigma Z(e)$ for any unit vector $e\\in H$ and $\\sigma\\ge0$.\n\nLet $X$ be a set, let $\\Theta\\subseteq X$ be a nonempty parameter set, let $K:X\\to H$ be a signal map, and let $\\epsilon>0$. For $x\\in\\Theta$ and $g\\in H$, define the observation functional\n\n$$Y_x(g)=\\langle Kx,g\\rangle_H+\\epsilon Z(g).$$\n\nThe associated statistical inverse experiment has sample space $\\mathbb R^H$ with the cylinder sigma-algebra generated by the evaluation maps $y\\mapsto y(g)$, and its sampling law $P_x$ is the law of the random function $g\\mapsto Y_x(g)$. Each sample is indexed by test functionals on $H$; it is not asserted to be a vector in $H$ or to have a continuous path. If $H=\\{0\\}$, linearity forces $Z(0)=0$ and the experiment is deterministic.",
          "strategy": "Define the functional Gaussian experiment on the cylinder sample space using a supplied isonormal process and its mean signal; do not identify observations with H-valued samples.",
          "deps": [
            "def-statistical-experiment",
            "def-hilbert-space",
            "def-random-element-and-real-random-variable",
            "def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions",
            "def-independent-random-elements",
            "lem-law-of-a-random-element-is-a-probability-measure"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Kretschmann and Werner, Maximum a posteriori testing in statistical inverse problems, §1.1, PDF pp. 1–2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.00686"
              },
              {
                "title": "Giné and Nickl, Mathematical Foundations of Infinite-Dimensional Statistical Models, §1.2.2, equations (1.6)–(1.8), PDF pp. 21–22",
                "url": "https://www.statslab.cam.ac.uk/~rn289/Site/__files/FULLPDF.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-ip-white-noise-spectral-sequence-representation",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "White-noise spectral sequence representation",
          "statement": "Let $H$ be a separable real Hilbert space with a fixed complete orthonormal basis $(u_j)$, indexed by $J=\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ for $m\\in\\mathbb N_0$ or by $J=\\mathbb N_{>0}$; the empty basis covers $H=\\{0\\}$. Let $Y_x(g)=\\langle Kx,g\\rangle_H+\\epsilon Z(g)$ be the isonormal white-noise experiment with $\\epsilon>0$, and write $\\mu_j=\\langle Kx,u_j\\rangle_H$. For every parameter $x$ and every basis index $j$, the observations $Y_x(u_j)$ are independent and have laws $N(\\mu_j,\\epsilon^2)$, where $N$ is the scalar normal law defined in the experiment.\n\nFor $g\\in H$, set $P_Ng=\\sum_{j\\in J,\\ j\\le N}\\langle g,u_j\\rangle_Hu_j$, with $P_N=I$ once all vectors of a finite basis have been included. Then\n\n$$Y_x(g)=L^2(\\mathbb P)\\text{-}\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\sum_{j\\in J,\\ j\\le N}\\langle g,u_j\\rangle_HY_x(u_j).$$\n\nThus every finite collection of observation functionals is reconstructed from the coefficient sequence in mean square. This does not assert almost-sure convergence of the full sequence of partial sums, a simultaneous path identity for all test vectors, or an $H$-valued observation.\n\nMore precisely, let $\\mathcal C_H$ be the cylinder sigma-algebra on $\\mathbb R^H$, let $\\mathcal C_J$ be the sigma-algebra on $\\mathbb R^J$ generated by its coordinate evaluations, and let $P_x$ be the field sampling law. Define $S:\\mathbb R^H\\to\\mathbb R^J$ by $S(y)_j=y(u_j)$ and $Q_x=S_*P_x$, where a pushforward is $S_*P_x(B)=P_x(S^{-1}(B))$. There is a measurable map $T:(\\mathbb R^J,\\mathcal C_J)\\to(\\mathbb R^H,\\mathcal C_H)$, depending only on the indexed basis, such that for every parameter $x$,\n\n$$S_*P_x=Q_x,\\qquad T_*Q_x=P_x,\\qquad S\\circ T=\\operatorname{id}_{\\mathbb R^J}.$$\n\nThese parameter-independent maps give equivalence of the field cylinder experiment and its coefficient experiment. One such map is the following. For $k\\ge1$ and $g\\in H$, let $N_k(g)$ be the least integer $N\\ge k$ with $\\|g-P_Ng\\|_H\\le2^{-k}$ and put\n\n$$a_{g,k}(z)=\\sum_{j\\in J,\\ j\\le N_k(g)}\\langle g,u_j\\rangle_Hz_j,\\qquad T(z)(g)=\\begin{cases}\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}a_{g,k}(z),&\\text{if the limit exists in }\\mathbb R,\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}$$\n\nFor each fixed $x$ and $g$, $T(S(Y_x))(g)=Y_x(g)$ almost surely, and the same holds jointly for each fixed finite list of test vectors. The exceptional event may depend on $x$ and on that list; no intersection over all $g\\in H$ is asserted. In dimension zero the coefficient space is a singleton and $T$ returns the field with value zero at $g=0$.\n\nIf $H=L^2([0,1])$ and the basis is one of the wavelet or trigonometric bases used to define the negative Sobolev carrier $H(\\delta)$, then under countable choice and for $\\delta>1/2$ the same coordinate sequence is a Borel Gaussian random element in $H(\\delta)$ with mean coefficients $Kx$ and noise level $\\epsilon$. In infinite dimension it is not an $L^2([0,1])$-valued sample.",
          "strategy": "Prove the Gaussian coordinate laws and L2 reconstruction; canonically select basis-tail subsequences, reconstruct evaluations measurably, and transfer probability laws on finite cylinders. Retain the qualified negative-Sobolev carrier clause.",
          "deps": [
            "def-ip-isonormal-white-noise-experiment",
            "def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
            "def-negative-sobolev-space-for-white-noise",
            "lem-white-noise-is-borel-gaussian-on-negative-sobolev-space",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "thm-chebyshev-markov-inequality-for-the-integral",
            "thm-first-borel-cantelli",
            "thm-sequential-suprema-infima-limsup-liminf-and-pointwise-limits-are-measurable",
            "lem-finite-measure-uniqueness-on-a-pi-system",
            "lem-law-of-a-random-element-is-a-probability-measure"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Kretschmann and Werner, Maximum a posteriori testing in statistical inverse problems, §1.1, PDF pp. 1–2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.00686"
              },
              {
                "title": "Giné and Nickl, Mathematical Foundations of Infinite-Dimensional Statistical Models, §§1.2.2–1.2.3 and isonormal construction, PDF pp. 21–22 and 483–484",
                "url": "https://www.statslab.cam.ac.uk/~rn289/Site/__files/FULLPDF.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Castillo and Nickl, Nonparametric Bernstein–von Mises theorems in Gaussian white noise, §1.2, equations (7)–(8), printed pp. 2005–2006",
                "url": "https://www.statslab.cam.ac.uk/~rn289/Site/__files/AOS1133.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Ding and Mathé, Minimax rates for statistical inverse problems under general source conditions, §1, equations (1)–(3), PDF pp. 11–12",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1707.01706"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
