        {
          "id": "def-ip-credible-set-and-frequency-coverage",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Posterior credibility and frequentist coverage",
          "statement": "Let $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$ be a measurable parameter space and, for each integer $n\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal E_n=((\\mathsf Y_n,\\mathcal Y_n),(P_{\\theta,n})_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$ be a statistical experiment. Let $\\Pi_{0,n}$ be a proper prior on $(\\Theta,\\mathcal T)$ and let $y\\mapsto\\Pi_n(\\cdot\\mid y)$ be a supplied posterior probability kernel under the associated prior-predictive law. For $0<\\gamma<1$, a data-indexed set rule $C_n:\\mathsf Y_n\\to\\mathcal P(\\Theta)$ is a posterior credible set at level $1-\\gamma$ if its membership graph $\\{(y,\\theta):\\theta\\in C_n(y)\\}$ is in $\\mathcal Y_n\\otimes\\mathcal T$ and $\\Pi_n(C_n(y)\\mid y)\\ge1-\\gamma$ for every $y$ in its declared data domain. At a fixed truth $\\theta\\in\\Theta$, its frequentist coverage is $c_n(\\theta):=P_{\\theta,n}\\{y:\\theta\\in C_n(y)\\}$. For a truth class $\\mathcal T_n\\subseteq\\Theta$, the rule is asymptotically honest at level $1-\\gamma$ on $\\mathcal T_n$ if $\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}\\inf_{\\theta\\in\\mathcal T_n}c_n(\\theta)\\ge1-\\gamma$, and it is asymptotically exactly calibrated there if $\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\mathcal T_n}|c_n(\\theta)-(1-\\gamma)|\\to0$.",
