          "id": "ex-ip-likelihood-free-conditional-flow-matching",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "Likelihood-free conditional flow matching without an MH correction",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let a proper prior and forward simulator define a joint prior-predictive law $\\pi$ for an unknown $X_1$ and data $Y$. Suppose the available interface can draw independent pairs $(X_1,Y)\\sim\\pi$ and reference states $X_0\\sim\\eta$, but supplies neither an evaluable likelihood density $L(x_1;y)$ nor an auxiliary nonnegative random estimate $\\widehat L(x_1;y,U)$ with $\\mathbb E_U\\widehat L(x_1;y,U)=L(x_1;y)$, where the expectation is over auxiliary randomness $U$. Conditional flow matching can train from these joint and reference samples and an independent $t\\sim\\operatorname{Unif}[0,1]$ using the linear path $X_t=(1-t)X_0+tX_1$ and target velocity $X_1-X_0$, without evaluating $L$. For a fixed query datum $y$, suppose the finite-dimensional prior has a finite-valued Lebesgue density $p_0$ with respect to the sigma-finite Lebesgue measure $\\lambda$ on the standard-Borel state space $\\mathbb R^d$ equipped with its Borel sigma-algebra. Assume the model has a finite-valued measurable likelihood density $L(\\cdot;y)$ at this datum, and set $\\widetilde p_y(x)=p_0(x)L(x;y)$. Require $0<Z_y:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\widetilde p_y(x)\\,\\lambda(dx)<\\infty$, so the posterior is a proper target with finite-valued density $p_y=\\widetilde p_y/Z_y$. Suppose the reference law $\\eta$ has a Lebesgue density and the learned flow satisfies the separate C$^1$ hypotheses of the ODE lemma; let $q_y$ be a finite-valued measurable version of the resulting proposal density, with $\\int q_y\\,d\\lambda=1$. Its independent proposal kernel is $Q_y(x,A):=\\int_Aq_y(z)\\,\\lambda(dz)$ for every $x$ and Borel $A$. For pairs with positive forward proposal-flow density $\\widetilde p_y(x)q_y(z)$, the density-based independent-Metropolis–Hastings acceptance probability is\n$$\\alpha_y(x,z)=\\min\\!\\left\\{1,\\frac{\\widetilde p_y(z)q_y(x)}{\\widetilde p_y(x)q_y(z)}\\right\\}.$$\nThe stated sampling interface does not in general supply this ratio, so it does not by itself give an evaluable density-based correction. In a specified special model the likelihood ratio may cancel or be otherwise known without separate likelihood evaluations; such a case needs no missing likelihood oracle. This example concerns the general limitation of the specified density-based correction; it does not rule out pseudo-marginal correction if an appropriate likelihood estimator is supplied, nor other correction methods with different inputs.",
