          "statement": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be real Hilbert spaces, let $D\\subseteq X$ be nonempty, and let $F:D\\to Y$ be a forward map. The domain $D$ is **weakly closed** when it is closed in the weak topology $\\sigma(X,X^*)$, the coarsest topology making every bounded real-linear functional on $X$ continuous. The graph of $F$ is **sequentially weak-to-weak closed** when, for every sequence $(x_n)$ in $D$ and every $x\\in X$ and $z\\in Y$, the conditions $x_n\\rightharpoonup x$ and $F(x_n)\\rightharpoonup z$ imply $x\\in D$ and $F(x)=z$; here weak convergence means convergence under every bounded real-linear functional on the corresponding Hilbert space. We call $F$ a **weakly closed forward map** when its domain is weakly closed and its graph is sequentially weak-to-weak closed. No linearity, norm continuity, Fréchet differentiability, or convexity is assumed; linear maps are allowed as special cases."
