          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Christian Clason, Regularization of Inverse Problems, §11.2, positive-noise discrepancy theorem",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.00617v2"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $U=\\mathbb R$, write $B_s(a):=\\{t\\in\\mathbb R:|t-a|<s\\}$, and let $F(t)=t$, exact solution $x^\\dagger=1$ with exact datum $y=1$, initial value $x_0=15/16$, noise bound $\\delta=0$ and datum $y^\\delta=1$, forcing fraction $\\sigma=1/2$, and discrepancy factor $\\tau=3$. For $n\\ge0$, define $h_n$ as the minimizer of\n$$h\\longmapsto\\frac12|h+x_n-1|^2+\\frac{\\alpha_n}{2}|h|^2$$\nfor some $\\alpha_n>0$ satisfying $|h_n+x_n-1|=\\sigma|x_n-1|$, and set $x_{n+1}=x_n+h_n$. The claim that this iteration reaches the zero discrepancy threshold $\\tau\\delta=0$ after finitely many updates is false. Indeed, $B_2(x_0)\\subset U$, $x^\\dagger\\in B_1(x_0)$, $|F'(t)|\\le1$, and for all $u,v\\in B_2(x_0)$,\n$$|F(u)-F(v)-F'(u)(u-v)|\\le|u-v|\\,|F(u)-F(v)|.$$\nAlso $e_0:=|x_0-x^\\dagger|=1/16<\\min\\{1,1/8,1/4\\}$, and $\\tau=3>1/\\sigma=2$."
