          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "statement": "The original scalar model is $F(c)=c$ with exact datum $y\\in\\mathbb R$. Relax it by adding an unconstrained variable $z\\in\\mathbb R$ and using $\\widetilde F(c,z)=c+z$, with penalty coordinate $P(c,z)=z$. The relaxed map satisfies the range-invariance factorization at $(0,0)$ with $K(a,b)=a+b$ and $r(c,z)=(c,z)$. For the exact datum, the unpenalized least-squares objective $J_0(c,z)=(c+z-y)^2$ has every pair with $c+z=y$ as a minimizer. For any fixed $\\lambda>0$, adding $\\lambda|P(c,z)|^2$ gives $J_\\lambda(c,z)=(c+z-y)^2+\\lambda z^2$, whose unique minimizer is $(y,0)$. This finite-dimensional example shows why an enlarged variable can require an explicit penalty; it asserts no general uniqueness result for other models.",
