        {
          "id": "def-ip-boundary-tv-conventions",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Full-space, Dirichlet, and Neumann TV conventions",
          "deps": [
            "def-ip-spatial-bv-and-distributional-tv"
          ],
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded Lipschitz domain, meaning a bounded open set whose boundary has a finite cover by coordinate neighborhoods in which it is the graph of a Lipschitz function and $\\Omega$ lies on one side of that graph. For $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$ define the Neumann total variation by $TV_N(u;\\Omega):=|Du|(\\Omega)$. Define its zero extension $\\widetilde u\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n)$ by $\\widetilde u=u$ almost everywhere on $\\Omega$ and $\\widetilde u=0$ almost everywhere on $\\mathbb R^n\\setminus\\Omega$. Define the Dirichlet total variation by $TV_D(u;\\Omega):=TV(\\widetilde u;\\mathbb R^n)$ when $\\widetilde u\\in BV(\\mathbb R^n)$ and by $TV_D(u;\\Omega):=+\\infty$ otherwise. For $v\\in BV(\\mathbb R^n)$ define the full-space total variation by $TV_F(v):=TV(v;\\mathbb R^n)$. Thus Neumann variation counts only the distributional derivative inside $\\Omega$, while Dirichlet variation also counts any jump created by zero extension across $\\partial\\Omega$.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "José A. Iglesias, Gwenael Mercier, and Otmar Scherzer, A note on convergence of total variation regularized linear inverse problems, Inverse Problems 34 (2018), 055011",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1711.06495"
              },
              {
                "title": "Gláucio Terra, Introduction to Geometric Measure Theory — Lecture Notes, version 1.6",
                "url": "https://www.ime.usp.br/~glaucio/mat6704/textos/GMTLecureNotes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 2
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-ip-bv-smooth-coarea",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Coarea identity for smooth scalar functions",
          "deps": [
            "def-ip-spatial-bv-and-distributional-tv",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "lem-ip-open-euclidean-locally-finite-smooth-partition",
            "lem-ip-unit-vector-density-has-scalar-variation",
            "lem-ip-lipschitz-graph-area-and-calculus-under-countable-choice",
            "lem-rat-embeds-dense",
            "thm-product-of-countable",
            "thm-rationals-countable",
            "thm-morse-sard-for-euclidean-maps",
            "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem",
            "cor-change-of-variables-for-compactly-supported-functions",
            "lem-local-graph-flux-from-fubini-and-the-fundamental-theorem",
            "lem-surface-integral-is-independent-of-c-one-boundary-charts",
            "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $u\\in C^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)\\cap L^1(\\Omega)$ satisfy $\\int_\\Omega|\\nabla u|\\,dx<\\infty$. For $t>0$ set $E_t^+:=\\{x\\in\\Omega:u(x)>t\\}$ and for $t<0$ set $E_t^-:=\\{x\\in\\Omega:u(x)<t\\}$. Then the level sets have finite perimeter relative to $\\Omega$ for almost every applicable $t$, and\n$$\\int_\\Omega|\\nabla u|\\,dx=\\int_0^\\infty P(E_t^+;\\Omega)\\,dt+\\int_{-\\infty}^0P(E_t^-;\\Omega)\\,dt.$$",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "BV Functions minicourse, Oxford Mathematical Institute, Version 3.0",
                "url": "https://www.maths.ox.ac.uk/system/files/attachments/BV_minicourse_Slides_Version3_0.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Gláucio Terra, Introduction to Geometric Measure Theory — Lecture Notes, version 1.6",
                "url": "https://www.ime.usp.br/~glaucio/mat6704/textos/GMTLecureNotes.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Piotr Rybka, The space in variational and evolution problems — Lecture Notes in Mathematical Sciences 15",
                "url": "https://www.ms.u-tokyo.ac.jp/publication/docs/lecturenotes15-rybka.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
