            "def-metric-space",
            "thm-lebesgue-differentiation-theorem-for-locally-integrable-functions-on-r-n"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $u\\in L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb R)$, with functions identified when they agree almost everywhere. For full-space TV, use $u$ itself. For a bounded-domain Dirichlet or Neumann problem, use the zero extension $\\widetilde u$ only to define the geometric level sets; this does not change the selected regularizer, and for each nonzero level the resulting set lies inside $\\Omega$. For $t>0$, the positive $t$-level class is the measurable-set class of $E_t^+(u):=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:u(x)>t\\}$; for $t<0$, the negative $t$-level class is the class of $E_t^-(u):=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:u(x)<t\\}$. These classes do not depend on the representative of $u$. For any measurable set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$, its density-one representative is\n\n$$E^{(1)}:=\\left\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:\\lim_{r\\downarrow0}\\frac{|E\\cap B(x,r)|}{|B(x,r)|}=1\\right\\}.$$\n\nThis representative depends only on the almost-everywhere class of $E$. The canonical level set $U_u^{(t)}$ is $(E_t^+(u))^{(1)}$ when $t>0$ and $(E_t^-(u))^{(1)}$ when $t<0$. Its canonical boundary is the ordinary topological boundary $\\partial U_u^{(t)}$ in $\\mathbb R^2$. For two nonempty boundary sets, use the usual extended Hausdorff distance induced by Euclidean distance; set $d_H(\\varnothing,\\varnothing)=0$ and $d_H(E,F)=+\\infty$ if exactly one set is empty. Hausdorff convergence uses this convention and is invariant under changes to a function on a null set.",
