            "thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space",
            "def-countable-choice",
            "def-ip-tv-functional-on-l2-distributional-variation",
            "lem-ip-tv-l2-functional-properties"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^2$, or let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ be a bounded Lipschitz domain. Set $X=L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ and let $Y=L^2(\\Sigma,\\mu;\\mathbb R)$ for a measure space $(\\Sigma,\\mu)$, using the real integral inner products; under Countable Choice these are real Hilbert spaces. Let $A:X\\to Y$ be bounded and real-linear, and let $A^*:Y\\to X$ be its Hilbert adjoint, characterized by $\\langle Au,p\\rangle_Y=\\langle u,A^*p\\rangle_X$. Define $R:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ as follows. If $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^2$, take $R(u)=TV_2(u;\\mathbb R^2)$, the supremum of $\\int u\\,\\operatorname{div}\\varphi$ over smooth compactly supported vector fields of Euclidean norm at most one. For bounded $\\Omega$, either take Neumann TV, $R(u)=TV(u;\\Omega)$ when $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$ and $+\\infty$ otherwise, or Dirichlet TV, $R(u)=TV(\\widetilde u;\\mathbb R^2)$ when the zero extension $\\widetilde u$ belongs to $BV(\\mathbb R^2)$ and $+\\infty$ otherwise. Thus the bounded-domain choices count, respectively, only the interior derivative or also the jump of the zero extension across the boundary. In the full-space case finite $R$ does not require global $L^1$ integrability.\n\nIdentify each $q\\in X$ with the continuous linear functional $h\\mapsto\\langle q,h\\rangle_X$ by the real Hilbert-space Riesz representation. For exact data $f=Au^\\dagger$, a **minimum-TV exact solution** is a point $u^\\dagger\\in X$ satisfying $Au^\\dagger=f$ and $R(u^\\dagger)\\le R(u)$ for every $u\\in X$ with $Au=f$. A vector $p^\\dagger\\in Y$ is a **dual source certificate** for $u^\\dagger$ if $A^*p^\\dagger\\in\\partial R(u^\\dagger)$, where $\\partial R$ is the convex subdifferential on the real Banach space $X$. The **TV source condition** at $u^\\dagger$ is that such a certificate exists; it does not assert uniqueness of the truth or of the certificate. For a chosen certificate, the associated Bregman distance is\n\n$$D_R^{A^*p^\\dagger}(u,u^\\dagger):=R(u)-R(u^\\dagger)-\\langle A^*p^\\dagger,u-u^\\dagger\\rangle_X,$$\n\nfor $u\\in\\operatorname{dom}R$.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "José A. Iglesias, Gwenael Mercier, and Otmar Scherzer, A note on convergence of solutions of total variation regularized linear inverse problems, Definition 1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1711.06495"
              },
              {
                "title": "Antonin Chambolle, Vincent Duval, Gabriel Peyré, and Clarice Poon, Geometric properties of solutions to the total variation denoising problem, §§1.3 and 3.1",
