          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $X=L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ and let $Y=L^2(\\Sigma,\\mu;\\mathbb R)$ be real Hilbert spaces. Let $A:X\\to Y$ be bounded and real-linear, let $f=Au^\\dagger$, and assume $u^\\dagger$ is an exact solution minimizing a proper, convex, lower semicontinuous, positively one-homogeneous TV functional $R:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ with a source certificate $p^\\dagger\\in Y$, so $A^*p^\\dagger\\in\\partial R(u^\\dagger)$. Use full-space $R=TV_2(\\cdot;\\mathbb R^2)$ if $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^2$; for a nonempty bounded connected Lipschitz $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$, use either Neumann $R(u)=TV(u;\\Omega)$ or Dirichlet $R(u)=TV(\\widetilde u;\\mathbb R^2)$ for the zero extension $\\widetilde u$, assigning $+\\infty$ outside the respective finite-variation domain. A bounded connected Lipschitz domain here is a nonempty bounded open set with a finite Lipschitz graph cover of its boundary, with the domain on one side of each graph, and with no separation into two disjoint nonempty relatively open sets.\n\nLet $\\alpha_n>0$ tend to zero, let $w_n\\in Y$, and suppose $\\|A^*\\|\\,\\|w_n\\|_Y/\\alpha_n\\le\\eta$ for some finite $\\eta$. For each $n$, suppose a supplied global minimizer $u_n\\in X$ minimizes\n$$J_n(u)=\\tfrac12\\|Au-(f+w_n)\\|_Y^2+\\alpha_nR(u).$$\nIn the Neumann case, if $A\\mathbf1_\\Omega=0$, take $u_n$ after subtracting its mean $|\\Omega|^{-1}\\int_\\Omega u_n$; this leaves $J_n$ unchanged. Then a subsequence converges weakly in $L^2(\\Omega)$ and, in addition, strongly in $L^1(\\Omega)$ when $\\Omega$ is bounded or strongly in $L^1$ on every bounded ball when $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^2$. Its limit $u_0$ is an exact solution $Au_0=f$ minimizing $R$ among exact solutions, and $A^*p^\\dagger\\in\\partial R(u_0)$.",
