      "order": 3066,
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-ip-admissible-constraint-class",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Admissible constraint classes and restricted identifiability",
          "deps": [
            "def-ip-forward-map-and-solution-classes",
            "def-linear-map",
            "def-kernel-and-image-of-a-linear-map"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "scraped": [],
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Christian Clason, Regularization of Inverse Problems, Chapter 3 §§3.1–3.2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.00617v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Tristan van Leeuwen and Christoph Brune, 10 Lectures on Inverse Problems and Imaging, Lecture 1 §1.2",
                "url": "https://tristanvanleeuwen.github.io/IP_and_Im_Lectures/what_is.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be real vector spaces, let $U\\subseteq X$ be nonempty, and let $A:X\\to Y$ be linear. Supply metrics $d_X$ on $X$ and $d_Y$ on $Y$; for a purely algebraic instance with no specified metrics, use the discrete metrics $d_X(x,x')=0$ if $x=x'$ and $1$ otherwise, and likewise on $Y$. These metrics require no compatibility with the vector operations, and no continuity of $A$ is assumed. The discrete triangle inequality holds because, if two endpoints differ, at least one of the two successive pairs differs. Give $U$ and its subsets their restricted metrics. Thus every pair of real vector spaces still admits this convention. The corresponding metric-space inverse-problem model has admissible truths $U$ and forward map $A|_U$. An **admissible constraint class** is a nonempty subset $C\\subseteq U$; its constrained forward map is the restriction $A|_C:C\\to Y$. The class $C$ is **identifiable for $A$** if $A|_C$ is injective, meaning $Ax=Ax'$ for $x,x'\\in C$ implies $x=x'$. Define the difference set and kernel by\n\n$$C-C:=\\{x-x':x,x'\\in C\\},\\qquad \\ker A:=\\{x\\in X:Ax=0_Y\\}.$$"
        },
        {
          "id": "prop-ip-constraints-can-remove-kernel-ambiguity",
          "kind": "proposition",
          "title": "A constraint removes kernel ambiguity exactly when its differences avoid the kernel",
          "deps": [
            "def-ip-admissible-constraint-class",
            "def-linear-map",
            "def-kernel-and-image-of-a-linear-map"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "justified_by": [],
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "scraped": [],
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Christian Clason, Regularization of Inverse Problems, Chapter 3 §§3.1–3.2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2001.00617v2"
              },
              {
                "title": "Tristan van Leeuwen and Christoph Brune, 10 Lectures on Inverse Problems and Imaging, Lecture 1 §1.2",
                "url": "https://tristanvanleeuwen.github.io/IP_and_Im_Lectures/what_is.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be real vector spaces, let $A:X\\to Y$ be linear, and let $C\\subseteq X$ be nonempty. The restriction $A|_C:C\\to Y$ is injective if and only if\n\n$$ (C-C)\\cap\\ker A=\\{0\\}. $$\n\nThis condition characterizes exact uniqueness on $C$; it gives no stability bound for perturbed data."
        },
        {
          "id": "alg-ip-stiefel-constrained-regularized-gradient",
          "kind": "algorithm",
          "title": "Finite-budget regularized gradient on a Stiefel constraint",
          "deps": [
            "def-ip-nonlinear-forward-map",
            "def-stiefel-grassmann-optimization-conventions",
            "thm-stiefel-grassmann-tangent-projections",
            "def-stiefel-qr-polar-retractions",
            "thm-stiefel-retractions-are-first-order"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "justified_by": [
            "lem-ip-stiefel-gradient-finite-budget-return-and-feasibility"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "scraped": [],
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "P.-A. Absil, R. Mahony, and R. Sepulchre, Optimization Algorithms on Matrix Manifolds, Chapter 3 (2008)",
                "url": "https://assets.press.princeton.edu/chapters/absil/Absil_Chap3.pdf",
                "locator": "Example 3.6.2: real Stiefel tangent space, induced Frobenius metric, and orthogonal projection."
              },
              {
                "title": "P.-A. Absil, R. Mahony, and R. Sepulchre, Optimization Algorithms on Matrix Manifolds, Chapter 4 (2008)",
                "url": "https://assets.press.princeton.edu/chapters/absil/Absil_Chap4.pdf",
                "locator": "Example 4.1.3: polar and QR retractions on the real Stiefel manifold."
              },
              {
                "title": "Nicolas Boumal, An Introduction to Optimization on Smooth Manifolds (2023 prepublication)",
                "url": "https://www.nicolasboumal.net/book/IntroOptimManifolds_Boumal_2023.pdf",
                "locator": "§§3.8 and 7.3: embedded-manifold gradients, Stiefel tangent/projection formulas, and the polar retraction."
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "For integers $1\\le p\\le n$, define the real Stiefel constraint set\n\n$$\\operatorname{St}(n,p):=\\{X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}:X^{\\mathsf T}X=I_p\\}.$$\n\nEquip $\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$ with the Frobenius inner product $\\langle U,V\\rangle_F=\\operatorname{tr}(U^{\\mathsf T}V)$ and norm $\\|U\\|_F=\\sqrt{\\langle U,U\\rangle_F}$. Let $H=\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$, let $Y$ be a real Hilbert space, let $U\\subseteq H$ be open and contain $\\operatorname{St}(n,p)$, and let $F:U\\to Y$ be continuously Fréchet differentiable. Given data $y\\in Y$, a reference matrix $X_{\\rm ref}\\in H$, and $\\lambda\\ge0$, define the residual and regularized objective by\n\n$$r(X):=\\|F(X)-y\\|_Y,\\qquad \\Phi(X):=\\tfrac12\\|F(X)-y\\|_Y^2+\\tfrac\\lambda2\\|X-X_{\\rm ref}\\|_F^2.$$\n\nSupply an ambient gradient oracle $G:U\\to H$ satisfying $D\\Phi(X)[Z]=\\langle G(X),Z\\rangle_F$ for every $X\\in U$ and $Z\\in H$, an initial frame $X_0\\in\\operatorname{St}(n,p)$, a residual tolerance $\\tau\\ge0$, a finite integer budget $K\\ge0$, and a step schedule $\\alpha_0,\\ldots,\\alpha_{K-1}$ with every $\\alpha_k>0$. For a square matrix $M$, put $\\operatorname{sym}(M):=(M+M^{\\mathsf T})/2$. The tangent-projection and metric-dual calculation for this $C^1$ objective, well-definedness of every tangent polar call, and finite-budget feasibility are justified by the finite-budget return and feasibility lemma. No smoothness beyond $C^1$ is required. The procedure is:\n\n1. Set the current frame to $X_0$ and the schedule index to $k=0$. If $r(X)\\le\\tau$, return $(X,r(X),\\texttt{target-met})$.\n2. While $k<K$, compute $G(X)$ and the Stiefel gradient $g:=G(X)-X\\operatorname{sym}(X^{\\mathsf T}G(X))$. If $g=0$, return $(X,r(X),\\texttt{stationary})$.\n3. Set $\\eta:=-\\alpha_k g$ and form the polar-retracted candidate\n\n   $$X_{\\rm trial}:=(X+\\eta)\\bigl((X+\\eta)^{\\mathsf T}(X+\\eta)\\bigr)^{-1/2}.$$\n\n4. Evaluate $\\Phi(X_{\\rm trial})$ and $r(X_{\\rm trial})$. If $\\Phi(X_{\\rm trial})>\\Phi(X)$, return $(X,r(X),\\texttt{objective-rejected})$. Otherwise accept $X_{\\rm trial}$ as the current frame. If its residual is at most $\\tau$, return $(X,r(X),\\texttt{target-met})$; otherwise increment $k$ by one and repeat the loop.\n5. When $k=K$, return $(X,r(X),\\texttt{budget-exhausted})$.\n\nThis procedure is restricted to the stated finite-dimensional real Stiefel constraint and the displayed polar retraction. It gives no convergence, global-optimality, or noisy-data stability guarantee, and it makes no finite-precision claim. It does not apply to positivity constraints or other nonlinear manifolds without a separately specified compatible retraction and proof."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-ip-stiefel-gradient-finite-budget-return-and-feasibility",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "The Stiefel gradient procedure returns a feasible iterate within its finite budget",
          "deps": [
            "alg-ip-stiefel-constrained-regularized-gradient",
