          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mikko Salo, RICAM lecture notes v1, §2.1: Radon transform in R²",
                "url": "https://users.jyu.fi/~salomi/lecturenotes/RICAM_notes_v1.pdf",
                "locator": "§2.1, pp. 4–7: line parameterization, Fourier convention, and full-angle backprojection."
              },
              {
                "title": "Frank Natterer, X-ray Tomography, in Inverse Problems and Imaging",
                "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/profile/Frank-Natterer/publication/226521751_X-ray_Tomography/links/02e7e525392792a0a3000000/X-ray-Tomography.pdf",
                "locator": "§2.1, pp. 18–20: Radon transform, Fourier slice theorem, and inversion; §3.1, pp. 26–28: filtered-backprojection discretization."
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Write $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C)$ for the complex-valued smooth functions of compact support on the Euclidean plane. For $u\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$, $n=1$ or $2$, use the compact-support Riemann Fourier transform\n$$\\widehat u(\\xi)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}u(x)e^{-2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi}\\,dx.$$\nFor $\\theta=(\\theta_1,\\theta_2)\\in S^1:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:|x|=1\\}$ put $\\theta^\\perp=(-\\theta_2,\\theta_1)$. The line with normal $\\theta$ and signed offset $s$ is $\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:x\\cdot\\theta=s\\}$. Its **Euclidean Radon transform** is\n$$Rf(\\theta,s):=\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(s\\theta+t\\theta^\\perp)\\,dt,\\qquad f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C),\\ (\\theta,s)\\in S^1\\times\\mathbb R.$$\nThe line integral is a compact-support Riemann integral.\nThe full data set uses every oriented normal $\\theta\\in S^1$ and every $s\\in\\mathbb R$; the same unoriented line is represented by $(\\theta,s)$ and $(-\\theta,-s)$.\nFor a one-dimensional function $h\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C)$, define\n$$|D_s|h(s):=\\int_{\\mathbb R}2\\pi|\\sigma|\\,\\widehat h(\\sigma)e^{2\\pi i s\\sigma}\\,d\\sigma.$$\nFor $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C)$ define the Riesz potential notation used here by\n$$|D|^{-1}f(x):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}e^{2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi}\\frac{\\widehat f(\\xi)}{2\\pi|\\xi|}\\,d\\xi.$$\nThe quotient in the last integral is assigned value $0$ at $\\xi=0$. These integrals are absolutely convergent on the stated classes: compact-support integration by parts gives rapid decay of $\\widehat f$ and $\\widehat h$, while $\\widehat f$ is bounded near $0$ and $|\\xi|^{-1}$ is locally integrable in two dimensions. Here the multiplier for $|D_s|$ is $2\\pi|\\sigma|$, corresponding to the $e^{-2\\pi i s\\sigma}$ Fourier convention."
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-ip-fourier-slice-identity",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "The Fourier-slice identity for the Euclidean Radon transform",
          "deps": [
            "thm-ftc-second-part",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
            "def-ip-2d-radon-transform",
