            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mikko Salo, RICAM lecture notes v1, §2.1: Radon transform in R²",
                "url": "https://users.jyu.fi/~salomi/lecturenotes/RICAM_notes_v1.pdf",
                "locator": "§2.1, pp. 4–7: the full-data filtered-backprojection identity."
              },
              {
                "title": "Frank Natterer, X-ray Tomography, in Inverse Problems and Imaging",
                "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/profile/Frank-Natterer/publication/226521751_X-ray_Tomography/links/02e7e525392792a0a3000000/X-ray-Tomography.pdf",
                "locator": "§3.1, pp. 26–28: filtering and backprojection steps and their finite-sampling context."
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and let $\\mu$ be the polar surface measure on $S^1$, with $\\mu(E)=2\\lambda_2(\\{r\\theta:\\theta\\in E,\\ 0<r\\le1\\})$ for Borel $E\\subseteq S^1$. The **complete-data filtered-backprojection procedure** takes as input the exact full sinogram $g=Rf$ of some $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C)$; all oriented normals $\\theta\\in S^1$ and all offsets $s\\in\\mathbb R$ are supplied. It returns the function $f_{\\mathrm{FBP}}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb C$ defined by\n$$f_{\\mathrm{FBP}}(x)=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{S^1}q(\\theta,x\\cdot\\theta)\\,d\\mu(\\theta),$$\nwhere the filter output is\n$$q(\\theta,s)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}2\\pi|\\sigma|\\,\\widehat{g(\\theta,\\cdot)}(\\sigma)e^{2\\pi i s\\sigma}\\,d\\sigma.$$\nThe exact instructions are: (1) for each $\\theta$, take the one-dimensional Fourier transform of $g(\\theta,\\cdot)$ with the negative-sign $2\\pi$ convention; (2) multiply that transform by the declared ramp filter $2\\pi|\\sigma|$ and take its inverse Fourier integral to form $q(\\theta,\\cdot)$; (3) evaluate $q(\\theta,\\cdot)$ at $s=x\\cdot\\theta$ and integrate over the full polar surface measure in $\\theta$; (4) divide by $4\\pi$. For this input class the slice identity and Schwartz decay make the filter integral absolutely convergent and the backprojection integral well defined. This definition specifies the continuous full-data procedure; it gives no guarantee for finitely sampled, truncated, or noisy measurements. Exact recovery on the stated class is proved by the normal-operator theorem."
        }
      ]
    }

