          "sources": {
            "references": []
          },
          "statement": "Let $\\eta(u)=0$ for $u\\le0$ and $\\eta(u)=e^{-1/u}$ for $u>0$, set $\\psi(x)=\\eta(1-|x|^2)$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, let $J=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\psi(x)\\,dx$, and define $\\phi=J^{-1}\\psi$. For $\\varepsilon>0$ put $\\phi_\\varepsilon(x)=\\varepsilon^{-2}\\phi(x/\\varepsilon)$. The functions $\\phi_\\varepsilon$ are radial smooth unit-mass point-source approximations. For $\\rho(s)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\phi(s\\theta+t\\theta^\\perp)\\,dt$, which is independent of $\\theta$, their full line data satisfy\n$$R\\phi_\\varepsilon(\\theta,s)=\\varepsilon^{-1}\\rho(s/\\varepsilon).$$\nFor each fixed $\\theta\\in S^1$ and every test function $h\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$, the offset distributions converge to the point mass:\n$$\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\\int_{\\mathbb R}R\\phi_\\varepsilon(\\theta,s)h(s)\\,ds=h(0).$$\nThus the point-source sinogram is $\\delta_0(s)$ in the offset variable, for every line normal."
