            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice for the continuous backprojection convention. As a finite-grid illustration, take eight equally spaced full-circle directions $\\theta_j=(\\cos(2\\pi j/8),\\sin(2\\pi j/8))$, $j=0,\\ldots,7$, four detector offsets $s_m=m-2$ for $m=0,1,2,3$, detector spacing $\\Delta s=1$, and the synthetic row $G_j=(0,0,1,0)$ at every direction. Apply the length-four radix-two FFT to each row, multiply frequency $k$ by the declared sampled ramp $\\lambda_k=2\\pi\\min(k,4-k)/4$, and invert the finite transform. Each filtered row is $Q=(0,-\\pi/4,\\pi/2,-\\pi/4)$. With the eight-point trapezoidal angular rule of weight $\\Delta\\theta=\\pi/4$, the one-pixel backprojection at $x=0$ is\n$$\\widetilde f(0)=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\Delta\\theta\\sum_{j=0}^7Q_j[2]=\\frac\\pi4.$$\nThis is the exact output of the specified finite computation. The array is a synthetic discrete sinogram; no claim is made that the finite detector or angle grid exactly represents a continuous phantom or that this output converges to one. The continuous theorem assumes full continuous data and makes no finite-sampling, truncation, or noise guarantee."
        }
