          ],
          "strategy": "Differentiate the compact-support Riemann integral in one complex coordinate. Bounded coordinates on a containing rectangle make the exponential difference quotients converge uniformly, so the Riemann integrals converge componentwise without a Lebesgue/Riemann identification or choice axiom.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mikko Salo, On geometric inverse problems and microlocal analysis, §2.1",
                "url": "https://users.jyu.fi/~salomi/pub/RICAM_notes_v1.pdf",
                "locator": "§2.1, pp. 4–5, Theorems 2.1–2.2: Radon line parameterization, Fourier-slice identity, and full-angle uniqueness by Fourier inversion; the coordinate-entire extension is proved here."
              },
              {
                "title": "Venkataswaran P. Krishnan and Eric Todd Quinto, Microlocal Analysis in Tomography",
                "url": "https://math.tifrbng.res.in/~vkrishnan/Papers/Microlocal_Analysis_Tomography.pdf",
                "locator": "§1.3.2, pp. 13–14, Theorem 3 proof: compact-support analytic continuation."
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C)$ and, for $z=(z_1,z_2)\\in\\mathbb C^2$, define $F(z)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}f(x)e^{-2\\pi i(x_1z_1+x_2z_2)}\\,dx$ using the compact-support Riemann integral on any rectangle containing $\\operatorname{supp}f$. This value is independent of the containing rectangle. Each coordinate slice of $F$ is entire, with $\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial z_j}(z)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}(-2\\pi ix_j)f(x)e^{-2\\pi i x\\cdot z}\\,dx$ for $j=1,2$, and the restriction of $F$ to $\\mathbb R^2$ is the compact-support Fourier transform in the stated line-normal convention. The proof uses no Countable Choice."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-ip-visible-covector-for-limited-angle-data",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Visible covectors for limited-angle data",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-ip-2d-radon-transform"
          ],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mikko Salo, On geometric inverse problems and microlocal analysis, §2.1",
                "url": "https://users.jyu.fi/~salomi/pub/RICAM_notes_v1.pdf",
                "locator": "§2.1, pp. 4–5: line-normal convention and Fourier-slice identity."
              },
              {
                "title": "Venkataswaran P. Krishnan and Eric Todd Quinto, Microlocal Analysis in Tomography",
                "url": "https://math.tifrbng.res.in/~vkrishnan/Papers/Microlocal_Analysis_Tomography.pdf",
                "locator": "§1.5.1, pp. 34–35: visible directions for restricted Radon data."
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $\\Theta\\subset S^1$ be a nonempty proper open set of line normals, assume $\\theta\\in\\Theta$ if and only if $-\\theta\\in\\Theta$, and assume $S^1\\setminus\\overline{\\Theta}$ contains a nonempty open arc. The measured line with normal $\\theta$ and signed offset $s$ is $\\ell_{\\theta,s}=\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^2:y\\cdot\\theta=s\\}$. Identify the nonzero cotangent vectors of the Euclidean plane with pairs $(x,\\xi)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\times(\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{0\\})$. Such a covector is visible from $\\Theta$ when $\\xi/|\\xi|\\in\\Theta$, and invisible when $\\xi/|\\xi|\\notin\\Theta$. The associated measured Fourier wedge is\n\n$$W_\\Theta=\\{\\sigma\\theta:\\theta\\in\\Theta,\\ \\sigma\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}\\}.$$"
        },
        {
