          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Take $\\Omega=B_2(0)$, $K=\\overline B_1(0)$, $\\Theta=\\{\\theta\\in S^1:|\\theta_1|<1/2\\}$, and $v=(1,0)$. Write $\\theta^\\perp=(-\\theta_2,\\theta_1)$ and define $R_\\Theta u(\\theta,s)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}u(s\\theta+t\\theta^\\perp)\\,dt$ for $\\theta\\in\\Theta$, $s\\in\\mathbb R$. Give the data space $D=L^2(\\Theta\\times\\mathbb R,d\\theta\\,ds)$ the norm $\\|g\\|_D=(\\int_\\Theta\\int_{\\mathbb R}|g(\\theta,s)|^2\\,ds\\,d\\theta)^{1/2}$, with arc-length measure $d\\theta$ and Lebesgue measure $ds$, and define $\\|u\\|_{H^1}=(\\|u\\|_2^2+\\|\\partial_1u\\|_2^2+\\|\\partial_2u\\|_2^2)^{1/2}$ using Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb R^2$. Then $\\Theta$ is open and antipodally symmetric, its complement contains open horizontal arcs, and $\\inf_{\\theta\\in\\Theta}|v\\cdot\\theta^\\perp|=\\sqrt3/2$. For every $M>0$, the class of smooth functions supported in $K$ with $H^1$ norm at most $M$ has no uniform Hölder stability estimate from the restricted Radon data. More explicitly, choose a real bump $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(B_1(0))$ with $\\|\\psi\\|_2=1$, set $\\lambda_M=M/[2(1+\\|\\nabla\\psi\\|_2+2\\pi)]$, and let $u_N(x)=\\lambda_MN^{-1}e^{2\\pi iNx_1}\\psi(x)$ for integers $N\\ge1$. These functions have the same compact support, satisfy $\\|u_N\\|_{H^1}\\le M$ and $\\|u_N\\|_2=\\lambda_MN^{-1}$, while for every integer $m\\ge1$ there is $C_m<\\infty$ with $\\|R_\\Theta u_N\\|_D\\le C_mN^{-m-1}$."
