          "statement": "Let $(M,g)$ be a compact, connected, nonempty smooth Riemannian surface with smooth boundary. Write $\\nu_{\\mathrm{out}}$ for its outward unit normal and use the curvature convention $R(A,B)C=\\nabla_A\\nabla_BC-\\nabla_B\\nabla_AC-\\nabla_{[A,B]}C$. Write $D_tJ=\\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma}J$; a Jacobi field along a unit-speed geodesic $\\gamma$ is a smooth field $J$ satisfying $D_t^2J+R(J,\\dot\\gamma)\\dot\\gamma=0$. Assume $g(\\nabla_w\\nu_{\\mathrm{out}},w)>0$ for every nonzero boundary-tangent vector $w$, no nonzero Jacobi field on any positive-length unit-speed geodesic segment vanishes at both endpoints, and every maximal unit-speed geodesic starting in $M^\\circ$ reaches $\\partial M$ in finite forward and backward time. Set $SM=\\{(x,v):x\\in M,\\ g_x(v,v)=1\\}$ and $\\partial_+SM=\\{(x,v)\\in SM:x\\in\\partial M,\\ g_x(v,-\\nu_{\\mathrm{out}})>0\\}$. For a real-valued $f\\in C_c^\\infty(M^\\circ,\\mathbb R)$ and $(x,v)\\in\\partial_+SM$, let $\\gamma_{x,v}$ be the unit-speed geodesic with initial data $(x,v)$, let $\\tau_+(x,v)$ be its first positive boundary-exit time, and define\n$$\nI_0f(x,v)=\\int_0^{\\tau_+(x,v)} f(\\gamma_{x,v}(t))\\,dt.\n$$\nThen $I_0f=0$ on $\\partial_+SM$ implies $f=0$ on $M$. No orientability assumption on $M$ is required."
