        {
          "id": "def-ip-pde-solver-admissibility-contract",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite-element PDE solver admissibility and certificate contract",
          "deps": [
            "def-ip-nonlinear-forward-map",
            "def-ip-nonlinear-tikhonov-functional",
            "def-matching-affine-simplicial-meshes",
            "def-reference-finite-element-and-unisolvent-dofs",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "thm-riesz-representation-in-finite-dimensions",
            "thm-nearest-point-in-a-nonempty-closed-convex-set"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Hannes Meinlschmidt, Optimization with Partial Differential Equations, complete lecture notes",
                "url": "https://hannes.meinlschmidt.eu/lecture_notes/pde_opt.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Michael Hinze, A Variational Discretization Concept in Control Constrained Optimization: The Linear-Quadratic Case",
                "url": "https://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/hinze/Psfiles/COAP0204.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Fernando Gaspoz, Christian Kreuzer, Andreas Veeser, and Winnifried Wollner, A Posteriori Error Analysis for Optimization with PDE Constraints",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.13170"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Fix an integer $d\\ge2$, a bounded polyhedral open set $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^d$, a finite matching affine simplicial mesh $\\mathcal T_h$ of $\\Omega$, and unisolvent reference finite elements assembled on that mesh. Thus the mesh is a finite face-to-face cover by nondegenerate affine simplices with disjoint interiors, and each local finite-element degree-of-freedom map is bijective on its finite-dimensional shape-function space. Fix coefficient spaces $\\Theta_h=\\mathbb R^m$, $V_h=\\mathbb R^n$, and $Y_h=\\mathbb R^s$ with their standard Euclidean inner products, where $m,n,s\\ge1$. Let $Q_h\\subseteq\\Theta_h$ be nonempty, closed, and convex, and let $O_h\\subseteq\\Theta_h$ be open with $Q_h\\subseteq O_h$.\n\nA **finite-element PDE model** in this contract consists of a continuously differentiable residual map $R_h:O_h\\times V_h\\to V_h$ and a continuously differentiable observation map $G_h:O_h\\times V_h\\to Y_h$. It must supply a continuously differentiable state branch $S_h:O_h\\to V_h$ satisfying $R_h(q,S_h(q))=0$ for every $q\\in O_h$. For every $q\\in Q_h$, this branch must be the specified unique admissible state and the matrix $D_uR_h(q,S_h(q))$ must be invertible. The discrete forward map is $F_h(q):=G_h(q,S_h(q))$. The unisolvent finite-element degrees of freedom identify $V_h$ with its coefficient space; all matrix transposes below are in the fixed Euclidean coefficient inner products.\n\nFor data $y^\\delta\\in Y_h$, reference parameter $q_0\\in\\Theta_h$, and $\\alpha>0$, the discrete nonlinear Tikhonov objective is\n$$\n\\Phi_h(q):=\\frac12\\|F_h(q)-y^\\delta\\|_{Y_h}^2+\\frac\\alpha2\\|q-q_0\\|_{\\Theta_h}^2\\qquad(q\\in Q_h).\n$$\nDefine the finite-dimensional objective before eliminating the state by\n$$\nJ_h(q,u):=\\frac12\\|G_h(q,u)-y^\\delta\\|_{Y_h}^2+\\frac\\alpha2\\|q-q_0\\|_{\\Theta_h}^2.\n$$\nThe symbols $\\nabla_uJ_h$ and $\\nabla_qJ_h$ denote the Euclidean gradient vectors representing the two partial derivative functionals. At an admissible $q$, the **exact discrete adjoint** $p_h(q)\\in V_h$ is the unique solution of\n$$\n\\bigl(D_uR_h(q,S_h(q))\\bigr)^T p_h(q)=\\nabla_uJ_h(q,S_h(q)).\n$$\nThe associated discrete-adjoint gradient vector is\n$$\ng_h(q):=\\nabla_qJ_h(q,S_h(q))-\\bigl(D_qR_h(q,S_h(q))\\bigr)^T p_h(q).\n$$\nThe next lemma proves that this vector is the gradient of $\\Phi_h$; the definition itself asserts no minimizer exists and no gradient iteration converges.\n\nAn **admissibility predicate** is a specified finite-time test $\\operatorname{adm}_h(q)$ that decides whether $q\\in Q_h$ and checks the model's stated coefficient, boundary, mesh, and time-step hypotheses before a PDE solve. For an explicit time discretization, a Courant–Friedrichs–Lewy condition (CFL condition) is a method-specific bound relating the time step to the mesh and the discrete evolution operator; no universal CFL threshold is part of this definition. A true predicate result certifies that the declared model hypotheses hold for that input, not merely that a heuristic check passed.\n\nThe model must also supply an exact finite-dimensional projection routine which returns $P_{Q_h}(z)$ for a queried $z\\in\\Theta_h$ in finite time or reports failure only when its declared finite call budget is exhausted. The unique nearest point exists because $Q_h$ is nonempty, closed, and convex, but an operational routine for computing it is an additional part of this contract.\n\nA solver routine with declared nonnegative state and adjoint tolerances $\\tau_u,\\tau_p$ and a finite solve budget must return either `failure` or approximate state and adjoint vectors $\\widetilde u,\\widetilde p$ together with certified finite bounds $\\rho_u,\\rho_p,\\eta_u,\\eta_p,P,L_{J,u},L_{R,u},M_q,L_{G,u}$. The residual bounds have the exact-system meaning $\\|R_h(q,\\widetilde u)\\|_{V_h}\\le\\rho_u$ and $\\|(D_uR_h(q,u))^T\\widetilde p-\\nabla_uJ_h(q,u)\\|_{V_h}\\le\\rho_p$, where $u=S_h(q)$; both must satisfy $\\rho_u\\le\\tau_u$ and $\\rho_p\\le\\tau_p$. In particular, an adjoint residual evaluated at $\\widetilde u$ alone does not certify $\\rho_p$ unless the model also bounds the resulting state and derivative-data defects. With $u=S_h(q)$ and $p=p_h(q)$, the model must certify\n$$\n\\|u-\\widetilde u\\|_{V_h}\\le\\eta_u,\\qquad \\|p-\\widetilde p\\|_{V_h}\\le\\eta_p,\\qquad \\|p\\|_{V_h}\\le P,\n$$\n$$\n\\|\\nabla_qJ_h(q,u)-\\nabla_qJ_h(q,\\widetilde u)\\|_{\\Theta_h}\\le L_{J,u}\\eta_u,\n$$\n$$\n\\|(D_qR_h(q,u))^T-(D_qR_h(q,\\widetilde u))^T\\|\\le L_{R,u}\\eta_u,\\qquad \\|(D_qR_h(q,\\widetilde u))^T\\|\\le M_q,\n$$\nand $\\|G_h(q,u)-G_h(q,\\widetilde u)\\|_{Y_h}\\le L_{G,u}\\eta_u$. It then returns\n$$\n\\widetilde g:=\\nabla_qJ_h(q,\\widetilde u)-(D_qR_h(q,\\widetilde u))^T\\widetilde p,\\qquad \\widetilde F:=G_h(q,\\widetilde u),\n$$\nAll constants and state/adjoint error bounds must be justified for the chosen model with every derivative-data, geometry, quadrature, and algebraic-solve defect retained. Certified residual bounds $\\rho_u,\\rho_p$ do not by themselves imply $\\eta_u,\\eta_p$; a separate model-valid stability estimate is required. The proposition below derives the resulting $\\varepsilon_g$ and $\\varepsilon_F$ bounds. An objective-evaluation routine must return in its finite budget either `failure` or an interval $[\\ell_h(q),u_h(q)]$ containing the exact value $\\Phi_h(q)$; `failure` is permitted only when a required state/adjoint solve fails its contract or the evaluation-call budget is exhausted. Exact evaluation is the degenerate-interval case. Projection and objective-evaluation calls consume the algorithm's finite evaluation budget. Each routine must return within its declared budget or report failure.\n\nIf a continuous model $F$ is also supplied, an optional mesh certificate $\\varepsilon_{\\rm mesh}$ must separately bound the claimed continuous-to-discrete error, such as $\\|F_h(q)-F(q)\\|$ or $\\|\\nabla\\Phi_h(q)-\\nabla\\Phi(q)\\|$. The state and adjoint algebraic residual certificates do not imply this mesh certificate."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-ip-finite-element-reduced-gradient-from-discrete-adjoint",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "The finite-element discrete adjoint gives the reduced gradient",
          "deps": [
            "def-ip-pde-solver-admissibility-contract",
            "def-euclidean-inner-product",
            "thm-riesz-representation-in-finite-dimensions",
            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "justified_by": [],
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Hannes Meinlschmidt, Optimization with Partial Differential Equations, complete lecture notes, §3.2",
                "url": "https://hannes.meinlschmidt.eu/lecture_notes/pde_opt.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Michael Hinze, A Variational Discretization Concept in Control Constrained Optimization: The Linear-Quadratic Case, §§1–3",
                "url": "https://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/hinze/Psfiles/COAP0204.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open and $V=\\mathbb R^n$ have its standard Euclidean inner product. Let $R:O\\times V\\to V$ and $G:O\\times V\\to Y$ be continuously differentiable, where $Y$ is a finite-dimensional real Euclidean space. Suppose $S:O\\to V$ is continuously differentiable, $R(q,S(q))=0$ for every $q\\in O$, and the matrix $D_uR(q,S(q))$ is invertible at a fixed $q\\in O$. Let $y\\in Y$, $q_0\\in\\mathbb R^m$, and $\\alpha>0$. Define\n$$\nJ(q,u):=\\frac12\\|G(q,u)-y\\|_Y^2+\\frac\\alpha2\\|q-q_0\\|_2^2,\\qquad \\Phi(q):=J(q,S(q)).\n$$\nLet $p\\in V$ be the unique solution of\n$$\n\\bigl(D_uR(q,S(q))\\bigr)^T p=\\nabla_uJ(q,S(q)),\n$$\nwhere $\\nabla_qJ$ and $\\nabla_uJ$ are the Euclidean representatives of the two partial derivative functionals. Then for every $h\\in\\mathbb R^m$,\n$$\nD\\Phi(q)[h]=\\left\\langle \\nabla_qJ(q,S(q))-\\bigl(D_qR(q,S(q))\\bigr)^T p,\\ h\\right\\rangle_{\\mathbb R^m}.\n$$\nConsequently $\\nabla\\Phi(q)=\\nabla_qJ(q,S(q))-(D_qR(q,S(q)))^Tp$. This is the exact derivative of the specified finite-dimensional model; it asserts no convergence of an iteration that changes $q$."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-ip-linear-finite-element-solve-residual-controls-state-error",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Certified inverse bounds turn finite-element solve residuals into state and adjoint error bounds",
          "deps": [
