          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "statement": "Let $M\\subset\\mathbb R^{d_a}$ be a compact smooth manifold of dimension $n\\ge d$, let $\\Omega\\subset(0,1)^d$ be a bounded domain, and let $\\sigma\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$ be globally Lipschitz and nonpolynomial. Fix finite input and output channel dimensions. A generalized Fourier neural operator is a map $\\Psi:L^2(M;\\mathbb R^{d_{in}})\\to L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^{d_{out}})$ of the form\n$$\\Psi=Q\\circ R\\circ F\\circ L_L\\circ\\cdots\\circ L_1\\circ E\\circ S.$$\nHere $S$ is a pointwise finite-dimensional affine network, $S(h)(x)=s(x,h(x))$; $E$ is a continuous linear chart-lifting map from manifold functions to multichannel functions on $\\mathbb T^n$; each $L_\\ell$ acts on periodic channel fields by a fixed matrix channel map plus a continuous periodic channel bias $b_\\ell(x)$ and a finite Fourier-multiplier convolution, followed coordinatewise by $\\sigma$; $F:L^2(\\mathbb T^n;\\mathbb R^{c})\\to L^2(\\mathbb T^d;\\mathbb R^{r})$ is a continuous linear dimension-reduction layer\n$$ (Fv)(x_1,\\ldots,x_d)=\\int_{\\mathbb T^{n-d}}K(z)v(x_1,\\ldots,x_d,z)\\,dz,$$\nwith a finite-dimensional matrix kernel $K$; $R$ is restriction of periodic fields to $\\Omega$; and $Q$ is a pointwise finite-dimensional output network. For the EIT boundary manifold $M=\\partial\\Omega\\times\\partial\\Omega$, the chart-lifting map $E$ may be taken to be the map $G$ of the chart-lifting lemma, with its finite chart-pair array treated as input channels. The finite Fourier multiplier at a layer is $\\widehat{\\mathcal K v}(k)=R_k\\widehat v(k)$ for finitely many $k\\in\\mathbb Z^n$, with real-valued outputs enforced by conjugate-symmetric coefficients. When $n=d$, take $c=r$ and $K=I_c$ on the one-point probability space $\\mathbb T^0$, so $F$ is the identity layer.\n\nThis is an architecture definition only. It asserts no approximation, training, statistical, discretization, or computational-cost guarantee."
