          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal H=L^2(\\partial\\Omega\\times\\partial\\Omega)$ and $\\mathcal Y=L^2(\\Omega)$ for a bounded smooth EIT domain. Given any nonempty finite sample set $S=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_N\\}\\subset\\mathcal H$, any $x_*\\in\\mathcal H\\setminus S$, and any $y_*\\in\\mathcal Y\\setminus\\{0\\}$, there are continuous maps $F_0,F_1:\\mathcal H\\to\\mathcal Y$ that agree at every sample input and differ by $y_*$ at $x_*$. Therefore exact fit on finitely many inputs alone cannot identify an arbitrary continuous inverse map. A continuum FNO approximation theorem concerns the full $L^2$ kernel input and $L^2$ conductivity output on a compact set; a finite-element experiment reports a finite discretization and finite train/test sample. Neither finite-data fit nor a discretized operator matrix supplies the continuum theorem's uniform bound or a statistical generalization guarantee."
