          "statement": "Let $n,m\\ge1$, $X=\\mathbb R^n$, and let $\\Omega$ be a Borel subset of $\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}\\times\\mathbb R^m$ with its relative Borel sigma-algebra. Equip $\\Omega\\times X$ with the finite product sigma-algebra $\\mathcal B(\\Omega)\\otimes\\mathcal B(X)$. Fix an integer $N\\ge0$, real parameters $\\tau_0,\\ldots,\\tau_{N-1}$, a Borel initialization $I:\\Omega\\to X$, Borel sets $D_k\\subseteq\\Omega\\times X$, and Borel maps $B_k:D_k\\to X$ for $0\\le k<N$, where each $D_k$ carries its relative product sigma-algebra. For $p=(A,y)\\in\\Omega$ and $x\\in X$, define $c(p,x)=A^{\\mathsf T}(y-Ax)$. Set $E_0=\\Omega$ and $x_0=I$; recursively, for $0\\le k<N$, set\n\n$$E_{k+1}:=\\{p\\in E_k:(p,x_k(p)+\\tau_k c(p,x_k(p)))\\in D_k\\},\\qquad x_{k+1}(p):=B_k(p,x_k(p)+\\tau_k c(p,x_k(p)))\\quad(p\\in E_{k+1}).$$\n\nThen every $E_k$ is a Borel subset of $\\Omega$ and every $x_k:E_k\\to X$ is Borel. In particular, $E_N$ is exactly the set of inputs where all $N$ updates are defined, and the finite output map $x_N:E_N\\to X$ is Borel."
