          },
          "axiom_use": "No choice axiom is required; the domains, maps, and deployment parameters are supplied explicitly.",
          "statement": "Let $n,m\\ge1$ be integers, $X=\\mathbb R^n$, and $Y=\\mathbb R^m$ with their standard Euclidean inner products and Borel sigma-algebras. Borel sets are members of the sigma-algebra generated by open sets, and a map is Borel if preimages of Borel sets are Borel. An instance is a pair $(A,y)$ in a declared Borel set $\\mathcal D\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}\\times Y$, where $A$ is the measurement matrix, $y$ is the observed data, and $A^\\mathsf T$ is the transpose matrix. For fixed $A$, the forward map is $F_A:X\\to Y$, $F_A(x)=Ax$, and the reconstruction target is the signal $x$ itself. For $x\\in X$, define the data-consistency correction by $c(A,y,x):=A^\\mathsf T(y-Ax)$.\n\nFor an integer depth $N\\ge1$, an unrolled regularization architecture consists of a Borel initialization map $I_\\theta:\\mathcal D\\to X$, parameters $\\theta=(\\theta_0,\\ldots,\\theta_{N-1})$ and real step parameters $\\tau_0,\\ldots,\\tau_{N-1}$ held fixed at deployment, and Borel update maps $B_{\\theta_k}:D_k\\to X$ with $D_k\\subseteq\\mathcal D\\times X$ Borel. Set $x_0=I_\\theta(A,y)$ and, for $k=0,\\ldots,N-1$, set\n\n$$x_{k+1}=B_{\\theta_k}\\bigl((A,y),x_k+\\tau_k c(A,y,x_k)\\bigr).$$\n\nFor fixed parameters and depth, let $\\mathcal D_{\\theta,N}$ be the Borel subset of $\\mathcal D$ for which the initialization and all $N$ recursive evaluations are defined. For $(A,y)\\in\\mathcal D_{\\theta,N}$, write $U_{\\theta_k}^{A,y}(x):=B_{\\theta_k}((A,y),x+\\tau_k c(A,y,x))$. The deployed map is\n\n$$\\Phi_{\\theta,N}(A,y)=\\bigl(U_{\\theta_{N-1}}^{A,y}\\circ\\cdots\\circ U_{\\theta_0}^{A,y}\\bigr)(I_\\theta(A,y)).$$\n\nAdmissibility requires this finite map to be Borel; it is a point reconstruction rule from the input pair $(A,y)$ to the signal target in $X$, not a probability-distribution-valued rule. The parameters may have been selected by any training procedure, and the architecture supplies no training-law specification or convergence, data-residual, or test-risk guarantee.\n\nAn exact proximal block is permitted when, for each fixed $(A,y)$, $B_{\\theta_k}((A,y),v)=\\operatorname{prox}_{\\alpha_k g}(v)$ for some $\\alpha_k>0$, where $g=g_{\\theta_k,(A,y)}:\\mathbb R^n\\to(-\\infty,+\\infty]$ has nonempty effective domain $\\{u:g(u)<+\\infty\\}$, satisfies $g((1-\\lambda)u+\\lambda v)\\le(1-\\lambda)g(u)+\\lambda g(v)$ for $u,v\\in\\mathbb R^n$ and $0\\le\\lambda\\le1$, and has closed epigraph $\\{(u,s):g(u)\\le s\\}$. Here $\\operatorname{prox}_{\\alpha g}(v)$ denotes the unique global minimizer $u\\in\\mathbb R^n$ of $g(u)+\\|u-v\\|_2^2/(2\\alpha)$. The proximal-map existence theorem supplies uniqueness under precisely these hypotheses. A general learned block is not assumed to be a proximal map or to minimize any objective.\n\nFor differentiation, fix an admissible data pair $(A,y)$ and regard the desired trainable parameter coordinates, including any step parameters to be differentiated, as a finite-dimensional input vector $\\eta$ in an open parameter set; hold the other values fixed. At the differentiation point $\\eta_0$, require an open neighborhood $V$ of $\\eta_0$ on which the actual initialization and all $N$ deployed updates are defined for this fixed $(A,y)$. Require the graph below to evaluate those same maps for every $\\eta\\in V$; a smooth extension beyond a declared Borel domain alone does not meet this requirement. Without such a neighborhood, no derivative of the deployed map is asserted. Construct a finite straight-line graph with input $\\eta$, initialization node $x_0=I_\\theta(A,y)$, and, for each $k=0,\\ldots,N-1$, distinct nodes\n\n$$v_k=x_k+\\tau_kA^{\\mathsf T}(y-Ax_k),\\qquad x_{k+1}=B_{\\theta_k}((A,y),v_k).$$\n\nIts output nodes are the coordinates of $x_N$. Thus each stage uses its own map $B_{\\theta_k}$ and step parameter $\\tau_k$; no common update map or shared layer parameter is required. Represent each initialization and update by its constituent primitive nodes or by a supplied complete deterministic $C^1$ primitive with its derivative rule; an opaque call without that map and rule is not an admissible graph primitive. Require every graph primitive to be continuously differentiable on an open neighborhood of its visited inputs. For a piecewise-$C^1$ primitive, each visited input must lie strictly inside a declared branch region, with that branch label fixed on an open neighborhood of the input; at a branch boundary or ambiguous branch selection the automatic-differentiation procedure returns no derivative. Apply forward-mode automatic differentiation to each scalar output-coordinate graph in the desired parameter direction. The forward-mode correctness theorem then identifies the returned tangent with the directional derivative of that finite output; coordinate directions give the Jacobian. When all stages instead repeat one $C^1$ map $G(z,\\theta)$ with a common parameter, the finite fixed-point-iteration procedure is the corresponding specialization. This differentiates only the deployed finite map and adds no assertion about an infinite iteration.\n\nFor $N\\ge3$, the diagram compresses the intervening states and updates into a single arrow; when $N=3$, that arrow is the single update $U_{\\theta_1}^{A,y}$. For $N=1$ and $N=2$, the recurrence above gives the corresponding one- and two-update chains. At one fixed admissible $(A,y)$, every arrow denotes finite function application, not a limit.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]\n\\node (data) at (0,1.1) {$(A,y)$};\n\\node (x0) at (0,0) {$x_0=I_\\theta(A,y)$};\n\\node (x1) at (3,0) {$x_1=U_{\\theta_0}^{A,y}(x_0)$};\n\\node (dots) at (5,0) {$x_{N-1}$};\n\\node (xn) at (8,0) {$x_N=\\Phi_{\\theta,N}(A,y)$};\n\\draw[->] (x0) -- node[above] {$U_{\\theta_0}^{A,y}$} (x1);\n\\draw[->] (x1) -- node[above] {$U_{\\theta_{N-2}}^{A,y}\\circ\\cdots\\circ U_{\\theta_1}^{A,y}$} (dots);\n\\draw[->] (dots) -- node[above] {$U_{\\theta_{N-1}}^{A,y}$} (xn);\n\\draw[->] (data) -- (x0);\n\\draw[->] (data) -- (x1);\n\\draw[->] (data) -- (dots);\n\\draw[->] (data) -- (xn);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```"
        },
