          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Giannis Daras et al., A Survey on Diffusion Models for Inverse Problems (2024), arXiv:2410.00083",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.00083"
              },
              {
                "title": "Yang Song et al., Score-Based Generative Modeling through Stochastic Differential Equations (2021), arXiv:2011.13456",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2011.13456"
              },
              {
                "title": "Simon J. D. Prince, Understanding Deep Learning, Chapter 18: Diffusion Models (2026), pp. 363–387; full course notes",
                "url": "https://csci4052u.science.ontariotechu.ca/generative_models/chapter_18_diffusion_models/notes.html"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$, let $\\sigma>0$, and let $\\mu$ be a probability measure on the Borel subsets of $\\mathbb R^d$. Write $\\varphi_\\sigma(v)=c_{\\sigma,d}\\exp(-\\|v\\|^2/(2\\sigma^2))$ for the normalized Gaussian density with respect to Lebesgue measure $dx$, define $P_\\sigma(du,dx)=\\mu(du)\\varphi_\\sigma(x-u)\\,dx$, and set $p_\\sigma(x)=\\int\\varphi_\\sigma(x-u)\\,\\mu(du)$. Define $Q_\\sigma(x,du)=\\varphi_\\sigma(x-u)\\mu(du)/p_\\sigma(x)$ and $m_\\sigma(x)=\\int u\\,Q_\\sigma(x,du)$; under $P_\\sigma$, write $X_0(u,x)=u$ and $X_\\sigma(u,x)=x$ for the coordinate random vectors, and set $Z=(X_\\sigma-X_0)/\\sigma$, so $Z\\sim N(0,I_d)$ is independent of $X_0$. For Borel maps $h,s:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ that are square-integrable with respect to $p_\\sigma(x)dx$, define $R(h)=\\int\\|h(x)-u\\|^2P_\\sigma(du,dx)$ and $J(s)=\\int\\|s(x)-(u-x)/\\sigma^2\\|^2P_\\sigma(du,dx)$. Then $p_\\sigma$ is positive and continuously differentiable, $Q_\\sigma$ is the conditional law of $X_0$ given $X_\\sigma=x$, and $\\nabla\\log p_\\sigma(x)=(m_\\sigma(x)-x)/\\sigma^2$ for every $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$. If $\\int\\|u\\|^2\\,\\mu(du)<\\infty$, $m_\\sigma$ uniquely minimizes $R$ up to $p_\\sigma$-almost-everywhere equality. The unique minimizer of $J$ is $\\nabla\\log p_\\sigma$, also up to $p_\\sigma$-almost-everywhere equality. Under the same Gaussian noising, suppose an observation variable $Y$ is conditionally independent of $Z$ given $X_0$, and, for the fixed observation $y$, $\\ell_y:\\mathbb R^d\\to(0,\\infty)$ is the bounded Borel conditional density of $Y$ at $y$ given $X_0=u$, relative to a fixed sigma-finite observation measure, and assume $0<Z_y=\\int\\ell_y(u)\\,\\mu(du)<\\infty$. Let $\\mu_y(du)=\\ell_y(u)\\mu(du)/Z_y$ and $p_\\sigma(x\\mid y)=\\int\\varphi_\\sigma(x-u)\\,\\mu_y(du)$. Then, for $r_\\sigma(y\\mid x)=\\int\\ell_y(u)\\,Q_\\sigma(x,du)$, the conditional score satisfies $\\nabla_x\\log p_\\sigma(x\\mid y)=\\nabla_x\\log p_\\sigma(x)+\\nabla_x\\log r_\\sigma(y\\mid x)$. No absolute-continuity or bounded-support assumption on $\\mu$ is required; $\\sigma>0$ supplies the full-support smoothing density.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "forward_refs": []
        },
        {
          "id": "def-ip-diffusion-prior-score-and-measurement-score",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Diffusion prior and measurement scores",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "lem-ip-conditional-score-and-tweedie-identities"
