              },
              {
                "title": "Yang Song et al., Score-Based Generative Modeling through Stochastic Differential Equations (2021), arXiv:2011.13456",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2011.13456"
              },
              {
                "title": "Hyungjin Chung et al., Diffusion Posterior Sampling for General Noisy Inverse Problems (2023), arXiv:2209.14687",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2209.14687"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $p_\\sigma$, $Q_\\sigma$, $m_\\sigma$, and $r_\\sigma(y\\mid x)$ be formed under the hypotheses of the conditional-score and Tweedie identities for Gaussian smoothing. The **diffusion prior score** is $s_\\sigma(x)=\\nabla_x\\log p_\\sigma(x)$, the exact score of the smoothed prior density. The **measurement score** is $g_\\sigma(x,y)=\\nabla_x\\log r_\\sigma(y\\mid x)$, where $r_\\sigma(y\\mid x)=\\int \\ell_y(u)\\,Q_\\sigma(x,du)$ is the likelihood density averaged over the conditional law of the clean state. For general inverse problems, evaluating this clean-state likelihood integral is generally computationally intractable. The **conditional score** is $c_\\sigma(x,y)=\\nabla_x\\log p_\\sigma(x\\mid y)$; it equals $s_\\sigma(x)+g_\\sigma(x,y)$, and the clean-state conditional mean is $m_\\sigma(x)=x+\\sigma^2s_\\sigma(x)$. A learned field $\\widehat s_\\theta$ is an estimate of $s_\\sigma$ and is exact only when it agrees with $s_\\sigma$ almost everywhere under $p_\\sigma(x)dx$. Replacing $r_\\sigma(y\\mid x)$ by the likelihood evaluated at $m_\\sigma(x)$ is a plug-in likelihood approximation, not part of the exact measurement-score definition.",
