          "statement": "Let $N\\ge1$ be finite, let $0=t_0<t_1<\\dots<t_N$, let $d\\ge1$ and let the state space be $\\mathbb R^d$, and let $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$ be a measurable observation space. Fix a measurable forward operator $\\mathcal A:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathsf Y$, an observation $y\\in\\mathsf Y$, and a nonnegative measurable likelihood function $\\ell_y:\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,\\infty)$ obtained by fixing $y$ in a likelihood density relative to a fixed sigma-finite measure on $\\mathsf Y$. A **measurement-guided reverse-kernel reconstruction procedure** takes a finite Borel score field $\\widehat s_\\theta:\\mathbb R^d\\times\\{t_0,\\dots,t_N\\}\\to\\mathbb R^d$; a terminal reference probability $\\nu_N$ on the Borel sets of $\\mathbb R^d$ and a sampler that returns an exact draw or failure within finite initialization budget $b_{\\mathrm{init}}$; and, for each $k$, a measurable correction space $(\\mathsf C_k,\\mathcal C_k)$, its failure-augmented measurable space $\\mathsf C_k^\\dagger=\\mathsf C_k\\sqcup\\{\\bot\\}$ with $\\sigma$-algebra $\\mathcal C_k^\\dagger=\\{D,D\\cup\\{\\bot\\}:D\\in\\mathcal C_k\\}$, a measurable correction map $C_{k,\\widehat s_\\theta,\\mathcal A,\\ell_y}:\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathsf Y\\to\\mathsf C_k^\\dagger$, a probability kernel $Q_{\\theta,k}(\\cdot\\mid x,y,a)$ from $\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathsf Y\\times\\mathsf C_k$ with its product sigma-algebra to $\\mathbb R^d$ with its Borel sigma-algebra, and a sampler that returns an exact draw from that kernel or failure within finite per-step budget $b_k$. The correction map may use the supplied score, forward operator and likelihood and, when supplied for the exact-score option, the prior and scale map. It may also take, for each step, a declared Borel map $\\Pi_{k-1}:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ and a Borel admissible set $A_{k-1}\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$. Each per-step budget includes correction evaluation, kernel sampling and any declared projection. First draw $x_N$ from $\\nu_N$ within $b_{\\mathrm{init}}$; if initialization fails, return the terminal-initialization failure status. For $k=N,N-1,\\dots,1$, compute $a_k=C_{k,\\widehat s_\\theta,\\mathcal A,\\ell_y}(x_k,y)$; if $a_k=\\bot$, return the correction failure status at $k$; otherwise draw $x_{k-1}$ from $Q_{\\theta,k}(\\cdot\\mid x_k,y,a_k)$ within $b_k$; if drawing fails, return the sampling failure status at $k$; if $\\Pi_{k-1}$ is supplied, apply it to $x_{k-1}$ and return the admissibility failure status at $k-1$ if evaluation fails or its output is outside $A_{k-1}$. If $k>1$, continue at $k-1$ with the resulting state $x_{k-1}$; if $k=1$, return $x_0$ with completed status. A correction may use a declared surrogate without additional likelihood or noising assumptions. If it uses the exact measurement score at step $k$, the caller must also declare a Borel probability measure $\\mu$ on $\\mathbb R^d$ and a map $\\sigma:\\{t_1,\\dots,t_N\\}\\to(0,\\infty)$ associating each reverse-schedule time with its Gaussian-noising scale. For every such step, the forward noising convention at $t_k$ must be $X_{\\sigma(t_k)}=X_0+\\sigma(t_k)Z$, where $X_0\\sim\\mu$ and $Z\\sim N(0,I_d)$ is independent of $X_0$, and the observation variable $Y$ with conditional density $\\ell_y$ must be conditionally independent of $Z$ given $X_0$, and $\\ell_y$ must be bounded, strictly positive and Borel measurable with $0<Z_y=\\int\\ell_y(u)\\,\\mu(du)<\\infty$. The exact correction then uses $g_{\\sigma(t_k)}(x_k,y)=\\nabla_x\\log\\int\\ell_y(u)Q_{\\sigma(t_k)}(x_k,du)$, with $Q_{\\sigma(t_k)}$ formed from this prior and Gaussian noising as in the conditional-score and Tweedie identities. The procedure returns an algorithmic sample; it makes no posterior-exactness claim unless the terminal law and all transition and projection conditions are separately certified.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]\n  \\node[draw,rounded corners,align=center] (init) at (0,0) {draw $x_N\\sim\\nu_N$ within $b_{\\mathrm{init}}$};\n  \\node[draw,rounded corners,align=center] (corr) at (3.5,0) {$a_k=C_k(x_k,y)$; check $a_k\\ne\\bot$};\n  \\node[draw,rounded corners,align=center] (kernel) at (7.5,0) {draw $x_{k-1}\\sim Q_{\\theta,k}(\\cdot\\mid x_k,y,a_k)$};\n  \\node[draw,rounded corners,align=center] (proj) at (11.5,0) {optional $\\Pi_{k-1}$; check $A_{k-1}$};\n  \\node[draw,diamond,aspect=2,align=center] (next) at (15.3,0) {$k=1?$};\n  \\node[draw,rounded corners,align=center] (out) at (15.3,-1.8) {return $x_0$};\n  \\node[draw,rounded corners,align=center] (stop) at (7.5,-2.2) {first failure status};\n  \\draw[->] (init)--(corr);\n  \\draw[->] (corr)--(kernel);\n  \\draw[->] (kernel)--(proj);\n  \\draw[->] (proj)--(next);\n  \\draw[->] (next.south)--node[right]{$k=1$}(out.north);\n  \\draw[->] (next.north) -- ++(0,1.5) -| node[pos=0.25,above,align=center]{$k>1$: continue at $k-1$\\\\with the just-produced state}(corr.north);\n  \\draw[->] (init.south)--(stop.north);\n  \\draw[->] (corr.south)--(stop.north);\n  \\draw[->] (kernel.south)--(stop.north);\n  \\draw[->] (proj.south)--(stop.north);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```\n\nThe upper return arrow repeats the reverse step with the newly produced state and decremented index. The downward arrows record immediate stopping at a failed initialization, correction, kernel draw, or declared projection. The diagram is an operational flow description and asserts no distributional identity.",
