          "statement": "Let $X_0$ take values $-1$ and $1$ with equal probability, let $X_\\sigma=X_0+\\sigma Z$ with $\\sigma>0$ and standard Gaussian noise independent of $X_0$, and let $Y=X_0+\\tau W$ for $W\\sim N(0,1)$ independent of $(X_0,Z)$, with $\\tau>0$. At the observation $y=0$, the exact measurement density $r_\\sigma(0\\mid x)=\\mathbb E[\\varphi_\\tau(-X_0)\\mid X_\\sigma=x]$ is constant in $x$, so its score is zero. The exact posterior mean is $m_\\sigma(x)=\\mathbb E[X_0\\mid X_\\sigma=x]=\\tanh(x/\\sigma^2)$, where $\\tanh(t)=(e^{2t}-1)/(e^{2t}+1)$. The plug-in density $\\varphi_\\tau(-m_\\sigma(x))$ has score $-m_\\sigma(x)m_\\sigma'(x)/\\tau^2$, which is nonzero for $x\\ne0$. Thus evaluating the measurement likelihood at the exact posterior mean does not in general recover the exact measurement score; this example does not rule out equality in special models.",
