          },
          "statement": "For each integer $m\\ge1$, let $\\mu_m=(1-2^{-m})\\delta_0+2^{-m}\\delta_1$ on $\\{0,1\\}\\subset\\mathbb R$, where $\\delta_a$ is unit mass at $a$, and let the target posterior be $\\pi=\\delta_0$. For example, this target is the posterior from prior $\\delta_0$ and the one-point observation model with likelihood one. The $m$th output law is realized by a one-step instance of the reverse-kernel interface with $N=1$, terminal law $\\nu_1=\\delta_0$, zero Borel score field, a constant correction into a singleton space, the constant kernel $Q_m(B\\mid x,y,a)=\\mu_m(B)$, and no projection. Its exact kernel sampler uses at most $m$ independent Bernoulli$(1/2)$ draws and returns $1$ exactly when all draws equal $1$, so it has finite budget for every $m$. The output laws $\\mu_m$ converge weakly to $\\pi$, yet $\\mu_m\\ne\\pi$ at every finite $m$ because $\\mu_m(\\{1\\})=2^{-m}$. This finite-kernel example illustrates the distinction between asymptotic weak convergence and exact posterior sampling; it is not a performance claim about a trained diffusion model.",
