          "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$, $m\\ge0$, $[m]=\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ with $[0]=\\varnothing$, and let $\\Phi=(a_i)_{i\\in[m]}$ be a finite family in $\\mathbb R^d$. Use the standard dot product $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\sum_{k<d}u_kv_k$, identify $x,z\\in\\mathbb R^d$ when $z=x$ or $z=-x$, and write $[x]$ for the resulting class. Define $\\mathcal I_\\Phi([x])=(\\langle a_i,x\\rangle^2)_{i\\in[m]}$, a formula independent of the representative because replacing $x$ by $-x$ leaves every square unchanged. For $S\\subseteq[m]$, let $\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}(\\Phi_S)$ be the set of finite real linear combinations of its vectors, with the empty span equal to $\\{0\\}$. Then $\\mathcal I_\\Phi$ is injective on the global-sign classes if and only if for every $S\\subseteq[m]$ at least one of $\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}(\\Phi_S)$ and $\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}(\\Phi_{[m]\\setminus S})$ equals $\\mathbb R^d$."
        },
