          "statement": "Let $A\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times d}$, $d\\ge1$, $m\\ge0$, with rows labeled $i=1,\\ldots,m$ and signal coordinates labeled $k<d$, and let $b:[m]\\to[0,\\infty)$. Denote row $i-1$ by $a_i$ and put $\\Phi=(a_i)_{i\\in[m]}$. Define $[x]$ using $x\\sim -x$ and $\\mathcal I_\\Phi([x])=((Ax)_i^2)_{i\\in[m]}$. Put $r_i=\\sqrt{b_i}$. Use the sign set consisting of the empty pattern when $m=0$ and otherwise the patterns $s:[m]\\to\\{-1,+1\\}$ with $s_1=+1$, ordered lexicographically with $+1$ before $-1$. For each pattern put $y_s=(s_i r_i)_{i\\in[m]}$, let $x_s=A^+y_s$ be the minimum-norm least-squares solution, and let $e_s=\\|Ax_s-y_s\\|_2$. Here $A^+$ is the unique Moore–Penrose inverse and $\\ker A=\\{v:Av=0\\}$. The algorithm processes patterns in order subject to a maximum pattern count $P\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ and signed-solve budget $L\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$; an early stop returns status budget-exhausted. In exact mode with tolerance $\\varepsilon=0$, after every pattern is processed it returns the union of the exact families $x_s+\\ker A$ modulo global sign, or reports infeasible if that union is empty. For every processed pattern, $x_s$ minimizes $\\|Ax-y_s\\|_2$, and the complete set of signed least-squares minimizers is $x_s+\\ker A$; this set solves $Ax=y_s$ exactly if and only if its residual $e_s$ is zero. The complete exact-mode output is exactly $\\{[x]:\\mathcal I_\\Phi([x])=b\\}$. In noisy mode the algorithm scores $J(x)=\\sum_{i\\in[m]}(|(Ax)_i|-r_i)^2$ and, after every canonical pattern is processed, returns a candidate minimizing $J$ over all $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$. When a budget stops enumeration early, the returned candidates describe only processed patterns and give no completeness or global-minimum guarantee. For $m=0$, the single pattern has the unique empty right-hand side and $J(x)=0$ for every $x$."
