          "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$, $m\\ge0$, and let $\\Phi=(a_i)_{i\\in[m]}$ be a finite family in $\\mathbb R^d$, with $[m]=\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ and $[0]=\\varnothing$. Write $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\sum_{k<d}u_kv_k$. If $m\\le2d-2$, then there are $x,z\\in\\mathbb R^d$ with $z\\ne x$ and $z\\ne-x$ but $\\langle a_i,x\\rangle^2=\\langle a_i,z\\rangle^2$ for every $i\\in[m]$. If $m\\ge2d-1$ and every list of $d$ distinct measurement labels indexes linearly independent vectors, then equality of all these squared measurements implies $z=x$ or $z=-x$. For every $d\\ge1$, this count is attained at $m=2d-1$: for any distinct real numbers $t_1,\\ldots,t_m$, the vectors $a_i=(1,t_i,t_i^2,\\ldots,t_i^{d-1})^{\\mathsf T}$ satisfy the stated independence condition and hence the squared measurements determine the signal up to global sign."
