          "statement": "Fix a coordinate orientation-reversing reflection $r:S^3\\to S^3$ for the mirror convention. Let $K$ and $J$ be smooth oriented knots in the standard oriented three-sphere. A connected-sum datum consists of one smoothly embedded closed three-ball around a short subarc of $K$ and one around a short subarc of $J$, each meeting its knot in a single trivial properly embedded arc, together with the fixed standard orientation-reversing boundary identification of the two standard oriented ball boundaries that joins the oriented arcs into one circle. Choose this standard map once in the standard two-ball model so that it commutes with $r$ and an orientation-preserving half-turn interchanging the two balls preserves the joining convention. Here a trivial arc is an embedded interval in a ball that is carried by an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism of ball-arc pairs to a straight diameter. Remove the interiors of the two balls and glue the remaining boundary spheres using this fixed identification; the resulting oriented knot in the standard oriented three-sphere is a connected sum of $K$ and $J$, denoted $K\\#J$ for the specified datum.\n\nThe **standard unknot** is the counterclockwise circle $U=\\{(x_1,x_2,0)\\in\\mathbb R^3:x_1^2+x_2^2=1\\}\\subset S^3=\\mathbb R^3\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$. Under inverse stereographic projection $x\\mapsto(2x/(1+\\lVert x\\rVert^2),(\\lVert x\\rVert^2-1)/(1+\\lVert x\\rVert^2))$, this is the unit circle in the first coordinate two-plane of the unit sphere in $\\mathbb R^4$. A knot is **nontrivial** if it is not ambient isotopic through orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms of $S^3$ to the standard unknot. A nontrivial knot is **prime** if it has no connected-sum presentation whose two summand knots are both nontrivial.",
