          "statement": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice when a knot's Seifert pairing is formed. An integral Seifert lattice is a pair $(H,V)$ in which $H$ is a free abelian group of rank $2g$ for some $g\\geq0$, and $V:H\\times H\\to\\mathbb Z$ is bilinear with $V-V^T$ unimodular. The rank-zero lattice is allowed, with the determinant of its empty skew matrix equal to $1$. A metabolizer is a primitive direct summand $M\\subset H$ of rank $g$ such that $V(x,y)=0$ for every $x,y\\in M$; the lattice is metabolic when it has a metabolizer. A chosen Seifert surface of an oriented knot gives such a lattice through the positive-push pairing. Its form is algebraically slice when that lattice is metabolic.\n\nAn isomorphism of integral Seifert lattices $(H,V)$ and $(H',V')$ is an integral group isomorphism $P:H\\to H'$ satisfying $V'(Px,Py)=V(x,y)$ for all $x,y\\in H$. Let $\\mathcal S$ be the commutative monoid of these isomorphism classes under orthogonal direct sum, where $(H,V)\\oplus(H',V')$ has underlying group $H\\oplus H'$ and pairing $V\\oplus V'$, with the rank-zero lattice as identity. The integral algebraic concordance group $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ is the abelian group generated by symbols $[V]$ for $[V]\\in\\mathcal S$, subject to $[V\\oplus W]=[V]+[W]$ and $[M]=0$ for every metabolic lattice $M$. Two selected Seifert forms are algebraically concordant when they represent the same element of $G_{\\mathbb Z}$. This form relation does not assert the existence of a smooth or locally flat topological concordance annulus between the knots."
