          "statement": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice when an integral Seifert lattice comes from a knot's positive-push pairing. For an integral Seifert lattice $(H,V)$, its integral Witt class is its class in $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ from the definition of metabolic Seifert form and algebraic knot concordance. An isomorphism $(W,B,T)\\to(W',B',T')$ of rational isometric structures is a rational-linear bijection $P:W\\to W'$ such that $B'(Px,Py)=B(x,y)$ and $PT=T'P$. A rational isometric structure is a triple $(W,B,T)$ in which $W$ is a finite-dimensional even-dimensional $\\mathbb Q$-vector space, $B:W\\times W\\to\\mathbb Q$ is a nonsingular symmetric bilinear form, $T:W\\to W$ is an invertible linear map satisfying $B(Tx,Ty)=B(x,y)$ for all $x,y\\in W$, and $I-T$ is invertible. Such a structure is metabolic if it has a $T$-invariant subspace $L\\subset W$ with $\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}L=\\frac12\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}W$ and $B|_{L\\times L}=0$. Define the rational Witt group $G_{\\mathbb Q}$ by direct sum of isomorphism classes, with metabolic structures set equal to zero. For a nonsingular integral Seifert matrix $V$, the associated matrix pair is $(B,T)=(V+V^T,V^{-1}V^T)$; whether this pair is a rational isometric structure is established separately.\n\nThe definition concerns finite-rank bilinear forms and isometry data. It does not identify the unrelated Witt Lie algebra with either $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ or $G_{\\mathbb Q}$."
