          "dependency_level": 1,
          "title": "Good fundamental groups for the disk-embedding theorem",
          "justified_by": [],
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Stefan Behrens et al., The Disc Embedding Theorem",
                "url": "https://dokumen.pub/the-disc-embedding-theorem-0198841310-9780198841319.html",
                "locator": "Definition 12.12 and Chapter 19, printed pp.180–181 and 273–282"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $\\Gamma$ be a group. Say that $\\Gamma$ is **good for the Freedman disk-embedding theorem** when it has the $\\pi_1$-null disk property: for every disk-like capped grope $G^c$ of height $1.5$ with a chosen basepoint and every homomorphism $\\phi:\\pi_1(G^c)\\to\\Gamma$, there is a proper framed generic immersion $d:D^2\\looparrowright G^c$ of a disk into the grope's actual four-dimensional framed block. Its boundary is the core attaching circle, with the normal framing specified by the attaching solid-torus neighborhood, and each double-point loop of $d$, regarded after basing as an element of $\\pi_1(G^c)$, is sent to the identity by $\\phi$. This definition of a good group is a hypothesis on $\\Gamma$; it does not assert that any particular group is good."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-freedman-casson-handle-skyscraper-and-surgery-modules",
