          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Michael H. Freedman and Frank Quinn, Topology of 4-Manifolds",
                "url": "https://archive.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/4789/1/FreedmanQuinn-TopologyOf4Manifolds-PMS39.pdf",
                "locator": "§§3.3, 3.8, printed pp.50–60; Chapter 4, printed pp.62–83, including §4.7, Proposition 4.7.1, printed pp.78–80"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stefan Behrens et al., The Disc Embedding Theorem",
                "url": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780198841319.001.0001",
                "locator": "Chapters 12–18 and 24–28: grope/tower construction and standardness"
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Powell, Arunima Ray and Peter Teichner, The4-dimensional disc embedding theorem and dual spheres",
                "url": "https://www.maths.gla.ac.uk/~mpowell/s00029-025-01069-y.pdf",
                "locator": "Selecta Mathematica31:80(2025), Lemmas3.3–3.4 and4.1–4.4, full Section5; smooth-input ordering in Section6"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $X$ be an oriented topological $4$-manifold with boundary, and let $V\\subset X$ be a connected relatively open neighborhood endowed with a specified smooth structure compatible with its smooth boundary collar and the given framed disk data, whose specified boundary collars and normal framings are smooth in this structure. Assume $\\pi_1(V)$ is good for disk embedding. Let $f_i:(D^2,S^1)\\looparrowright(V,V\\cap\\partial X)$ be a finite proper smooth generic disk datum with pairwise disjoint embedded framed boundaries, and framed smooth immersed spheres $g_i$ satisfying $\\lambda(f_i,g_j)=\\delta_{ij}$, $\\lambda(g_i,g_j)=0$, $\\mu(g_i)=0$, $\\lambda(f_i,f_j)=0$ for $i\\ne j$, and $\\mu(f_i)=0$, computed in $\\mathbb Z[\\pi_1(V)]$.\n\nThere is a finite family of proper framed one-story capped-grope towers in $V$, of first-story height at least four, whose attaching regions are the Whitney circles for a regular homotopy of the $f_i$. Their bodies extend to embedded convergent skyscraper pinched neighborhoods, each with a relative standard-two-handle parameterization preserving its attaching solid torus and framing. The corresponding embedded framed Whitney disks give pairwise disjoint proper locally flat disks $\\bar f_i$ homotopic to $f_i$ rel boundary and framing. They carry product normal neighborhoods supplied by this construction and framed immersed geometric duals $\\bar g_i$ homotopic in $V$ to $g_i$.\n\nIn particular one may take $V=X$ when $X$ and the given maps, boundary collars and framings are smooth. No existence of a smoothing of a general topological neighborhood is asserted. The towers are capped-grope skyscrapers; an unrestricted Casson-handle construction is not assumed."
        },
        {
