          "dependency_level": 1,
          "justified_by": [],
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $W$ be a compact connected oriented smooth four-manifold whose boundary $M$ is a rational homology three-sphere, meaning $H_*(M;\\mathbb Q)\\cong H_*(S^3;\\mathbb Q)$. The boundary intersection lattice has underlying group $L_W=H_2(W;\\mathbb Z)/\\operatorname{Tor}H_2(W;\\mathbb Z)$. Poincaré–Lefschetz duality identifies each $x\\in H_2(W;\\mathbb Z)$ with a class $\\alpha_x\\in H^2(W,M;\\mathbb Z)$. Define $Q_W(\\bar x,\\bar y)=\\langle\\alpha_x\\smile\\alpha_y,[W,M]\\rangle$, where $\\smile$ is the relative cup product and $[W,M]\\in H_4(W,M;\\mathbb Z)$ is the relative orientation class; equivalently, this is the signed intersection number of interior two-cycle representatives. Its rank is $b_2(W)=\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}H_2(W;\\mathbb Q)$. A nondegenerate positive-definite $Q_W$ is called a positive-definite filling lattice. It is not required to be unimodular; the boundary linking form can make its determinant have absolute value greater than one.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "strategy": "not-applicable",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Allen Hatcher, Algebraic Topology",
                "url": "https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf",
                "locator": "§3.3, Poincaré–Lefschetz duality for manifolds with boundary and cup-product pairing, printed pp. 252–255"
              }
            ]
          },
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable"
        },
