          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For an oriented two-component link $L=L_1\\cup L_2$, let $\\operatorname{lk}(L_1,L_2)$ be one half the sum of the signs of all crossings between distinct components, using $\\operatorname{sgn}\\det(v_{\\mathrm{over}},v_{\\mathrm{under}})$ in the oriented projection plane with positive viewing normal. Extend the singular finite-difference formula to links by resolving marked crossings independently. At one marked mixed crossing, the positive-minus-negative derivative of $\\operatorname{lk}$ is $+1$; reversing the order of those two resolutions gives $-1$. A marked self-crossing has derivative $0$. At any two distinct marked crossings, mixed or self, the second derivative is $0$, so linking number is a type-at-most-one invariant of two-component links and, by the nonzero first derivative, has exact type one. The total pairwise linking number of a one-component knot is $0$, because the defining sum over distinct component pairs is empty."
