          "statement": "Assume a Conway skein function $\\nabla$ on oriented links exists with $\\nabla_U=1$ and $\\nabla_{L_+}-\\nabla_{L_-}=z\\nabla_{L_0}$. For a knot $K$, put $a_2(K)=[z^2]\\nabla_K(z)$. The unframed degree-one chord-diagram module is zero. The degree-two symbol of $a_2$ is the weight system $w_2$ that assigns $1$ to the intersecting two-chord diagram and $0$ to the diagram with two nonintersecting chords. In particular, $a_2(U)=0$ for the unknot and $a_2(T)=1$ for the positive trefoil $T$ that is the closure of the two-strand braid $\\sigma_1^3$. Let $\\widetilde w_2:\\mathcal A_2^{\\mathrm{un}}\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\mathbb C\\to\\mathbb C$ be the complex-linear extension of $w_2$. Under countable choice, the degree-two truncation of the normalized Kontsevich series recovers this example up to its unique degree-zero normalization: for every knot type $K$,\n$$\na_2(K)=\\widetilde w_2(I_2(K))-\\widetilde w_2(I_2(U)).\n$$\nHere $I_2(K)$ is the degree-two coefficient of $I(K)$, and $I_0(K)=1$ is its degree-zero coefficient. This calculation is conditional on the existence supplier for $\\nabla$ and asserts no rationality for the coefficients of $I(K)$.\n\nThe two degree-two chord patterns are shown below. Endpoints alternate in the first diagram and not in the second; the diagram only labels the two cases used in the algebraic check.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9,thick]\n  \\begin{scope}\n    \\draw (0,0) circle (1);\n    \\draw[->] (0.1,0.99) .. controls (0.35,0.95) and (0.52,0.84) .. (0.65,0.76);\n    \\draw[very thick] ({cos(60)},{sin(60)}) -- ({cos(240)},{sin(240)});\n    \\draw[very thick] ({cos(150)},{sin(150)}) -- ({cos(330)},{sin(330)});\n    \\node at (0,-1.35) {$D_{\\times}$};\n  \\end{scope}\n  \\begin{scope}[xshift=3.4cm]\n    \\draw (0,0) circle (1);\n    \\draw[->] (0.1,0.99) .. controls (0.35,0.95) and (0.52,0.84) .. (0.65,0.76);\n    \\draw[very thick] ({cos(40)},{sin(40)}) -- ({cos(130)},{sin(130)});\n    \\draw[very th