          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "justified_by": [],
          "statement": "Let a finite planar resolution cube be equipped with an arrow at each crossing, transported to its saddle edges while resolving the other disjoint crossing disks, and use the odd exterior merge and split maps. There is a function assigning a sign to every edge such that the resulting signed differential squares to zero over the integers. More precisely, one can prescribe the face products on all faces, including faces whose two composites vanish, as follows. On a face with nonzero composites let $\\kappa\\in\\{1,-1\\}$ be their relative commutation sign. On a zero-composite face the two saddle arcs have alternating endpoints on one circle, one arc inside and one outside; put them in the standard two-chord picture by an orientation-preserving planar isotopy and rotate so that the inside arrow points upwards and set $\\kappa=1$ when the outside arrow points left, and $\\kappa=-1$ when it points right. Then the prescribed product of the four edge signs is $-\\kappa$. These prescriptions are consistent, and two edge assignments realizing the same prescriptions are related by a vertexwise sign change. All the constructions are finite and choice-free. If a point away from the saddle disks is marked on a component of the diagram, the vertexwise comparison commutes with its marked right $\\mathbb Z[X]/(X^2)$-action.",
