          "statement": "Let $G$ and $G'$ be one-component grid diagrams related by one of the following local moves. A cyclic permutation changes the planar cut of the toroidal grid by moving the first row or column to the last. A row commutation exchanges consecutive rows whose closed horizontal marking intervals are disjoint or strictly nested; a column commutation uses the same condition on vertical marking intervals. A stabilization replaces a marked cell by a two-by-two block with the original marking type in two opposite cells and the other type in a third cell, leaves the fourth cell empty, and inserts one row and one column, relocating the old companion markings so each new row and column has one mark of each type. Any of the four empty corners is allowed, and destabilization is the inverse move. Regard each full filtered minus complex returned by the grid-complex algorithm as a complex over $\\mathbb F_2[U]$ by letting $U$ act as one chosen marking variable $U_i$, retaining the other independent variables. The resulting complexes are filtered chain homotopy equivalent over $\\mathbb F_2[U]$, with equivalences preserving the Maslov grading and Alexander filtration. For each move, finite grid-domain counts give filtered chain maps in both directions and filtered chain homotopies from both composites to the identity. For a stabilization, its multivariable target is first compared with the cone of $U_{n+1}+U_i$ on the polynomial extension of the original complex. After instead taking the simultaneous ordinary quotient $U_1=\\cdots=U_n=U$, a stabilization contributes a zero-map cone, equivalently an extra zero-differential factor $V$; those fully collapsed complexes are compared with this factor retained. Under commutation the tilde homologies are isomorphic; under a stabilization from grid number $n$ to $n+1$, the tilde homology changes by tensoring with the two-dimensional bigraded vector space $V$ with one generator in bidegree $(0,0)$ and one in bidegree $(-