        {
          "id": "def-balanced-taut-sutured-manifold-and-product-region",
          "kind": "definition",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "title": "Balanced taut sutured manifolds and characteristic product regions",
          "justified_by": [],
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yi Ni, Knot Floer homology detects fibred knots, arXiv:math/0607156v4, §§2.1 and 6.1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0607156v4",
                "locator": "Printed pp.3–5 and 16–17; sutured manifolds, product disks and annuli, horizontal surfaces, vertical primeness, and characteristic product region"
              },
              {
                "title": "Andras Juhasz, Floer homology and surface decompositions, arXiv:math/0609779v3, §§2 and 9",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0609779v3",
                "locator": "Printed pp.3–5 and 31–38; balanced and taut sutured manifolds, horizontal decompositions, and the rank-one/product criterion"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "A **sutured manifold** $(M,\\gamma)$ consists of a compact oriented $3$-manifold $M$ with boundary and a union $\\gamma$ of pairwise disjoint annuli and tori in $\\partial M$. Each annulus contains a suture, its oriented core circle; write $A(\\gamma)$ for the annuli and $s(\\gamma)$ for the union of their cores. Orient each component of $R(\\gamma)=\\partial M\\setminus\\operatorname{int}(\\gamma)$ so that its boundary orientation agrees in the annulus with the homology class of the suture. The regions whose normal points out of $M$ form $R_+(\\gamma)$ and those whose normal points into $M$ form $R_-(\\gamma)$. The sutured manifold is **balanced** when $M$ has no closed component, $\\chi(R_+)=\\chi(R_-)$, and every component of $\\partial M$ meets an annular component of $\\gamma$. It is **irreducible** when every embedded $2$-sphere in $M$ bounds a $3$-ball in $M$. A properly embedded surface $S$ is **incompressible** when it has no compressing disk. A compressing disk is an embedded disk $D\\subset M$ with $\\partial D\\subset S$, $D\\cap S=\\partial D$, and $\\partial D$ an essential simple closed curve in $S$ (that is, it does not bound a disk in $S$). For a compact surface $S$ put $x(S)=\\sum_{S_j}\\max(0,-\\chi(S_j))$, where the sum is over its connected components. The Thurston norm $x(\\alpha)$ of a relative homology class $\\alpha\\in H_2(M,\\gamma;\\mathbb Z)$ is the minimum of $x(S)$ over properly embedded oriented representatives $S$ with $\\partial S\\subset\\gamma$ and $[S]=\\alpha$. Such a representative is **norm-minimizing** when it is incompressible and $x(S)=x(\\alpha)$. A balanced sutured manifold is **taut** when it is irreducible and $R(\\gamma)$ is norm-minimizing in $H_2(M,\\gamma;\\mathbb Z)$.\n\nA **product sutured manifold** is $(R\\times[0,1],\\partial R\\times[0,1])$ for a compact oriented surface $R$, with positive and negative regions $R\\times\\{1\\}$ and $R\\times\\{0\\}$. A properly embedded oriented surface $S\\subset M$ is a **decomposing surface** when every component $\\lambda$ of $S\\cap\\gamma$ is either a properly embedded nonseparating arc in an annular component meeting its suture once, a simple closed curve in an annular component homologous to that suture, or a homotopically nontrivial curve in a toroidal component, with all such curves in one torus component representing the same homology class. A **product disk** is a decomposing surface that is a disk and meets the sutures in exactly two points. A **product annulus** is a decomposing annulus with one boundary circle in $R_+$ and one in $R_-$. A **horizontal surface** $S$ in a balanced sutured manifold is a decomposing surface with no closed component, $|\\partial S|=|s(\\gamma)|$, $[S]=[R_+]$ in $H_2(M,\\gamma;\\mathbb Z)$, and $\\chi(S)=\\chi(R_+)$. The sutured manifold is **vertically prime** when every horizontal surface is parallel to $R_+$ or $R_-$, where parallel means that the surfaces cobound an embedded product between them.\n\nWhen $(M,\\gamma)$ is irreducible, $\\gamma$ has no torus component, and $R_+$ and $R_-$ are incompressible and diffeomorphic, a **product region** is a submanifold $\\Phi\\times[0,1]\\subset M$ for a compact possibly disconnected surface $\\Phi$ such that $\\Phi\\times\\{0\\}$ and $\\Phi\\times\\{1\\}$ are incompressible subsurfaces of $R_-$ and $R_+$, respectively. A **characteristic product region** is a product region $E\\times[0,1]$ such that every product region $\\Phi\\times[0,1]$ can be carried into it by an ambient isotopy of $M$."
        },
