        {
          "id": "def-sutured-floer-chain-complex",
          "kind": "definition",
          "deps": [
            "def-balanced-taut-sutured-manifold-and-product-region"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "title": "Sutured Floer chain complexes and homology notation",
          "justified_by": [
            "lem-ni-holomorphic-disk-and-jsj-analytic-inputs"
          ],
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Andras Juhasz, Floer homology and surface decompositions, arXiv:math/0609779v3, §§2–3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0609779v3",
                "locator": "Printed pp.3–11; balanced sutured diagrams, admissibility, the chain complex, and relative Spin^c structures"
              },
              {
                "title": "Yi Ni, Knot Floer homology detects fibred knots, arXiv:math/0607156v4, §2.2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0607156v4",
                "locator": "Printed pp.5–7; the sutured Floer invariant associated to a sutured manifold and its diagram-independence construction"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "For a balanced sutured manifold $(M,\\gamma)$, a **balanced sutured Heegaard diagram** is a tuple $\\mathcal H=(\\Sigma,\\boldsymbol\\alpha,\\boldsymbol\\beta)$ where $\\Sigma$ is a compact oriented surface with boundary and each of $\\boldsymbol\\alpha,\\boldsymbol\\beta$ is a finite family of pairwise disjoint simple closed curves in $\\operatorname{int}\\Sigma$. It defines $M$ by attaching $3$-dimensional $2$-handles to $\\Sigma\\times[0,1]$ along $\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\times\\{0\\}$ and $\\boldsymbol\\beta\\times\\{1\\}$, with sutures $\\partial\\Sigma\\times\\{1/2\\}$ on $\\gamma=\\partial\\Sigma\\times[0,1]$. The diagram is balanced when the two families have equal cardinality $d$ and the natural maps from components of $\\partial\\Sigma$ to components of $\\Sigma\\setminus\\bigcup\\boldsymbol\\alpha$ and $\\Sigma\\setminus\\bigcup\\boldsymbol\\beta$ are both surjective. A **domain** is an integral two-chain generated by the closures of regions of $\\Sigma\\setminus(\\bigcup\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cup\\bigcup\\boldsymbol\\beta)$ disjoint from $\\partial\\Sigma$. A **periodic domain** is a domain whose boundary is an integral sum of full $\\alpha$- and $\\beta$-curves. The diagram is **admissible** when each nonzero periodic domain has at least one positive and one negative region coefficient.\n\nThe symmetric product $\\operatorname{Sym}^d(\\Sigma)=\\Sigma^d/S_d$ is the space of unordered $d$-tuples of points. The product tori $\\mathbb T_\\alpha,\\mathbb T_\\beta$ are the images of the products of the $d$ attaching curves in this symmetric product. Require curves from different families to intersect transversely. A **generator** is a point of $\\mathbb T_\\alpha\\cap\\mathbb T_\\beta$, equivalently a collection of $d$ transverse intersection points, one on each attaching curve in each family. A **Whitney disk class** from generator $\\mathbf{x}$ to generator $\\mathbf{y}$ is a relative homotopy class of maps of a disk into $\\operatorname{Sym}^d(\\Sigma)$ whose two boundary arcs lie on the product tori and whose endpoints are $\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y}$; $\\pi_2(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})$ denotes the set of these classes. For $d>0$, fix a smooth path $J_t$ of almost complex structures on $\\operatorname{Sym}^d(\\Sigma)$ for which the product tori are totally real and which is regular for the disk classes used in the differential and its squared-differential argument. Here an almost complex structure is a smooth bundle endomorphism $J$ with $J^2=-\\operatorname{id}$, and regularity means that the linearized Cauchy--Riemann operator at every nonconstant holomorphic strip in those classes is surjective; in particular impose this for Maslov indices one and two. The counts below use such regular data, obtained by generic perturbation within the sutured Floer analytic setup. A representative of a Whitney disk class is a smooth strip $u:\\mathbb R\\times[0,1]\\to\\operatorname{Sym}^d(\\Sigma)$ satisfying $\\partial_su+J_t(u)\\partial_tu=0$, with $u(s,0)\\in\\mathbb T_\\alpha$, $u(s,1)\\in\\mathbb T_\\beta$, and limits $\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y}$ as $s\\to-\\infty,+\\infty$. Its Maslov index is the Fredholm index of the linearized Cauchy--Riemann operator at a representative, and $\\mathcal M_J(\\phi)$ denotes the space of representatives of class $\\phi$. Translation of the real coordinate gives the $\\mathbb R$ action on this moduli space.\n\nWhen $d=0$, $\\operatorname{Sym}^0(\\Sigma)$ is a point, both product tori are that point, and the unique generator is the empty collection.\n\nFor a fixed admissible diagram and the chosen regular data, the **sutured Floer disk-count module** $SFC(\\mathcal H;R)$ is the free $R$-module on its generators, where $R=\\mathbb F_2$ or $\\mathbb Z$. For $R=\\mathbb F_2$, its formal differential counts translation classes modulo two:\n$$\\partial\\mathbf{x}=\\sum_{\\mathbf{y}}\\sum_{\\substack{\\phi\\in\\pi_2(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})\\\\\\mu(\\phi)=1}}\\#_2\\bigl(\\mathcal M_J(\\phi)/\\mathbb R\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf{y}.$$\nFor $R=\\mathbb Z$, use a coherent orientation system, which assigns orientations to determinant lines of the linearized disk operators compatibly with gluing, and replace $\\#_2$ by the signed count $\\#_{\\mathfrak o}$. The homology of this diagram-level differential is denoted $H_*(SFC(\\mathcal H;R))$ once the moduli counts are finite and the differential squares to zero. When $d=0$ the unique empty generator has zero differential. Once admissible diagram-change maps are proved to be chain homotopy equivalences, their common homology is denoted $SFH(M,\\gamma;R)$. This definition fixes the chain-level and coefficient conventions; it does not assert analytic compactness, gluing, invariance, or surface-decomposition results."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-knot-relative-spinc-structure",
          "kind": "definition",
          "deps": [
            "def-balanced-taut-sutured-manifold-and-product-region",
            "def-sutured-floer-chain-complex"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "title": "Relative Spin-c structures and Chern classes for sutured knot exteriors",
          "justified_by": [],
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Andras Juhasz, Holomorphic discs and sutured manifolds, §4",
                "url": "https://msp.org/agt/2006/6-3/agt-v6-n3-p16-s.pdf",
                "locator": "Printed pp.1441–1442, connected-manifold convention and Definition 4.2; applied componentwise here"
              },
              {
                "title": "Andras Juhasz, Floer homology and surface decompositions, arXiv:math/0609779v3, §3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0609779v3",
                "locator": "Printed pp.6–9; boundary vector fields, sutured Spin-c structures, relative Chern classes, and generator assignment"
              },
              {
                "title": "Yi Ni, Knot Floer homology detects fibred knots, arXiv:math/0607156v4, §§1–2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0607156v4",
                "locator": "Printed pp.2 and 5–7; knot Floer Alexander summands and sutured diagrams for a null-homologous knot"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $(M,\\gamma)$ be a balanced sutured manifold. Fix a nowhere-zero vector field $v_0$ along $\\partial M$ that points into $M$ on $R_-(\\gamma)$, points out of $M$ on $R_+(\\gamma)$, and on each annular suture region follows the gradient of a height coordinate whose level circles are the sutures. A **sutured Spin-c structure** is a homology class of nowhere-zero vector fields $v$ on $M$ whose boundary restriction is $v_0$. Two such fields are homologous when, in every connected component $M_i$ of $M$, their restrictions outside some open ball in $\\operatorname{int}M_i$ are homotopic through nowhere-zero fields fixed on $\\partial M_i$; for $M=\\varnothing$ this condition is vacuous. The set of these classes is denoted $\\operatorname{Spin}^c(M,\\gamma)$.\n\nFor a balanced Heegaard diagram of $(M,\\gamma)$ and a generator $\\mathbf{x}$, choose a Morse function compatible with the diagram. The generator specifies the multi-trajectory joining its paired index-one and index-two critical points. Modify the gradient in a regular neighborhood of that trajectory to obtain a nowhere-zero vector field agreeing with $v_0$ on $\\partial M$; for this chosen Morse data its homology class is denoted $\\mathfrak{s}(\\mathbf{x})$. The theorem that the assignment is independent of auxiliary Morse and gradient choices is part of diagram invariance, not asserted by this definition. The chain subcomplex generated by generators assigned to a fixed class $\\mathfrak{s}$ has homology denoted $SFH(M,\\gamma,\\mathfrak{s};R)$ for coefficients $R$ whenever the diagram-level differential is defined. For a chosen trivialization $t$ of the oriented plane field $v_0^\\perp$ along $\\partial M$, the relative Chern class $c_1(\\mathfrak{s},t)\\in H^2(M,\\partial M;\\mathbb Z)$ is the relative Euler obstruction to extending $t$ over $v^\\perp$, where $v$ represents $\\mathfrak{s}$ and the plane orientation is determined by the orientation of $M$ and the direction of $v$.\n\nFor an oriented null-homologous knot $K$ in a closed connected oriented $3$-manifold $Y$, let $M=Y\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ and let $\\gamma_\\mu$ be the union of two disjoint annuli on $\\partial M$ whose core sutures are parallel meridians of $K$ with opposite orientations. A Seifert surface is regarded in $M$ after deleting a collar of its boundary, so it determines a relative class on the sutured knot exterior. The meridional direction supplies the boundary trivialization of $v_0^\\perp$ used to normalize the relative Chern-class pairing."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-top-alexander-grading-of-knot-floer-homology",
          "kind": "definition",
          "deps": [
            "def-balanced-taut-sutured-manifold-and-product-region",
            "def-sutured-floer-chain-complex",
            "def-knot-relative-spinc-structure"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "title": "The absolute Alexander grading of knot Floer homology",
          "justified_by": [],
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Yi Ni, Knot Floer homology detects fibred knots, arXiv:math/0607156v4, §1",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0607156v4",
                "locator": "Printed p.2; null-homologous knots, Seifert classes, integral Alexander summands, and top grading"
              },
              {
                "title": "Peter Ozsvath and Zoltan Szabo, Holomorphic disks and knot invariants, arXiv:math/0209056v2, §3.4",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0209056v2",
                "locator": "Printed p.18; the Alexander summand via half the Chern-class pairing with the capped Seifert surface"
              },
              {
                "title": "Ciprian Manolescu, An Introduction to Knot Floer Homology, §§3.3–3.5 and 4.1–4.3",
                "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/~cm5/hfk.pdf",
                "locator": "Printed pp.12–18; pointed diagrams, absolute grading conventions, and the S3 normalization"
              },
              {
                "title": "Ciprian Manolescu, Peter Ozsvath, and Sucharit Sarkar, A combinatorial description of knot Floer homology, arXiv:math/0607691v2, §§1–3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0607691v2",
                "locator": "Printed pp.1–18; explicitly mod-two grid complexes and their Alexander grading"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $Y$ be a closed connected oriented $3$-manifold, let $K\\subset Y$ be a smooth oriented null-homologous knot, and let $F\\subset Y$ be a compact connected oriented embedded surface with oriented boundary $K$. Choose a closed tubular neighborhood $N(K)$, put $M=Y\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$, and let $\\gamma_\\mu$ be the union of two disjoint annuli on $\\partial M$ whose core sutures are parallel meridians of $K$ with opposite orientations. Delete a collar of $\\partial F$ to regard $F$ as a properly embedded surface in $(M,\\gamma_\\mu)$, with relative class $[F]\\in H_2(M,\\partial M;\\mathbb Z)$. Let $t_\\mu$ be the meridional boundary trivialization of the oriented plane field used in the relative Spin-c definition. For $\\mathfrak{s}\\in\\operatorname{Spin}^c(M,\\gamma_\\mu)$, its Alexander grading is\n\n$$A_F(\\mathfrak{s})=\\frac12\\left\\langle c_1(\\mathfrak{s},t_\\mu),[F]\\right\\rangle,$$\n\nwith the standard sign convention that gives the usual Alexander grading for knots in $S^3$. For a balanced admissible sutured Heegaard diagram of $(M,\\gamma_\\mu)$, the Spin-c summand $SFH(M,\\gamma_\\mu,\\mathfrak{s};\\mathbb Z)$ is the homology of the integral sutured Floer disk-count complex generated by the Heegaard generators assigned to $\\mathfrak{s}$. Define the integral knot Floer group in Alexander grading $i$ by\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathrm{HFK}}(Y,K,[F],i;\\mathbb Z)=\\bigoplus_{\\substack{\\mathfrak{s}\\in\\operatorname{Spin}^c(M,\\gamma_\\mu)\\\\ A_F(\\mathfrak{s})=i}}SFH(M,\\gamma_\\mu,\\mathfrak{s};\\mathbb Z).$$\n\nThis fixes the integral coefficient convention. The equivalent associated-graded closed Heegaard-complex presentation, invariance under changing diagrams, and comparison with the mod-two grid complex are separate theorems; this definition supplies none of their proofs or a coefficient-change map."
        },
        {
