          "statement": "Let $G$ be any group and $\\rho,\\rho':G\\to SL_2(\\mathbb C)$ be representations with $$\\operatorname{tr}\\rho(g)=\\operatorname{tr}\\rho'(g)\\qquad(g\\in G).$$ If $\\rho$ is irreducible, then $\\rho'$ is irreducible and there is $P\\in SL_2(\\mathbb C)$ such that $\\rho'(g)=P\\rho(g)P^{-1}$ for every $g$. If $\\rho$ is reducible, it has the same trace character as its diagonal semisimplification $g\\mapsto\\operatorname{diag}(\\chi(g),\\chi(g)^{-1})$, where $\\chi:G\\to\\mathbb C^*$ is the character on a common invariant line. The two diagonal characters $\\chi,\\chi^{-1}$ determine the same semisimple conjugacy class. This is a pointwise statement; it does not assert that an irreducible character component admits a tautological representation over its function field or prove rational quotient descent.",
