          "statement": "Let $a>b\\ge2$ be coprime integers, choose integers $\\alpha,\\beta$ with $a\\alpha+b\\beta=1$, and put $G=\\langle x,y\\mid x^a=y^b\\rangle$, $h=x^a=y^b$, $\\mu=y^\\alpha x^\\beta$ and $\\lambda=h\\mu^{-ab}$. Consider all irreducible representations $\\rho:G\\to SL_2(\\mathbb C)$, where irreducible means having no common invariant complex line. The common peripheral eigenvalue pairs $(M,L)\\in(\\mathbb C^*)^2$ have reduced Zariski closure with vanishing ideal $$(1+M^{2a}L)\\quad\\hbox{if }b=2,\\qquad (M^{2ab}L^2-1)\\quad\\hbox{if }b>2.$$ More precisely, $\\rho(h)=\\epsilon I$ with $\\epsilon=-1$ when $b=2$ and $\\epsilon\\in\\{1,-1\\}$ when $b>2$; each permitted sign occurs in a family realizing every point of the graph $L=\\epsilon M^{-ab}$ except possibly finitely many meridian values. The displayed ideal is the ideal of the entire irreducible eigenvalue locus, not just of the exhibited families. No choice axiom is assumed.",
