          "statement": "Let $A$ be a commutative integral domain with fraction field $F$. Let $B:A^s\\to A^n$, $C:A^n\\to A^t$ and $U=(u,v):A^2\\to A^n$ be finite matrices with $CB=CU=0$. Put $\\beta=\\operatorname{rank}_F B$, $\\gamma=\\operatorname{rank}_F C$, and assume $$\\operatorname{rank}_F(B\\mid U)=\\beta+1.$$ Then $h=n-\\gamma-\\beta\\ge1$. There exists $0\\ne f\\in A$ such that, over $A_f$, the kernel, image and homology $H_f=\\ker C_f/\\operatorname{im}B_f$ have explicit free bases, with ranks $n-\\gamma$, $\\beta$ and $h$. The kernel $$L_f=\\ker\\{A_f^2\\xrightarrow{U_f}H_f\\}$$ is a free rank-one direct summand of $A_f^2$. In explicit homology coordinates the images of $u,v$ can be written $a e_1,b e_1$, with at least one of $a,b$ a unit of $A_f$. Hence $$L_f=A_f(-b,a).$$ All these assertions, including the displayed kernel, commute with any homomorphism $A_f\\to E$ to a field. In particular they describe the actual homology kernel of the specialized matrices, not just a kernel before specialization.\n\nIf $A=\\mathbb C[X]$ for a nonempty irreducible affine algebraic set $X$, this gives a regular projective kernel line $[-b:a]$ on a nonempty dense principal open subset and hence a rational map $X\\dashrightarrow\\mathbb P^1$. Its slope convention $-\\text{second coordinate}/\\text{first coordinate}$ is the projective value $[a:b]$, including zero and infinity. The rank-chart expressions agree wherever both are defined. The kernel line is unchanged by invertible changes of the three chain bases, by adding boundary columns to $U$, and by multiplying both columns of $U$ by the same invertible scalar. For rational changes it is unchanged after restricting to a common open set where their denominators and inverses are defined. No assertion that the matrix rank hypotheses hold for an arbitrary topological or representation-theoretic family is part of this lemma.",
