          "statement": "Assume full AC. Let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot with chosen compact exterior $E_K$ and boundary torus $T_K=\\partial E_K$. A simple closed curve on a surface is **essential** if it is not null-homotopic there. All surfaces considered here are compact. A surface $S\\subset E_K$ is properly embedded if $S\\cap\\partial E_K=\\partial S$ and its interior lies in the interior of $E_K$. It is **incompressible** if it has no embedded disk with interior disjoint from $S$ and essential boundary in $S$; it is **boundary-incompressible** if there is no embedded disk $D\\subset E_K$ with $\\partial D=\\alpha\\cup\\beta$, where $\\alpha,\\beta$ are arcs meeting exactly at their common endpoints, $D\\cap S=\\alpha$, $D\\cap\\partial E_K=\\beta$, and $\\alpha$ does not cobound a disk in $S$ with an arc of $\\partial S$. A **boundary slope** of $K$ is the unoriented isotopy class of an essential simple closed curve on $T_K$ that occurs as a boundary component of a connected, properly embedded, orientable, incompressible, boundary-incompressible surface. Using the positive meridian $\\mu$ and preferred longitude $\\lambda$ as a homology basis, a primitive class $a[\\lambda]+b[\\mu]$ has slope $b/a\\in\\mathbb Q\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, with $a=0$ interpreted as $\\infty$. A boundary slope is **strict** if it has a connected realizing orientable, incompressible, boundary-incompressible surface which is not a fiber of any locally trivial fibration $E_K\\to S^1$; a fiber is the inverse image of a point under that bundle projection.",
