          "statement": "Assume full AC. Let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot, $E_K$ its exterior, and $\\rho:\\pi_1(E_K)\\to SL_2(\\mathbb C)$ a representation. The **adjoint local system** $\\operatorname{Ad}\\rho$ has fibre $\\mathfrak{sl}_2(\\mathbb C)$ and left group-module action $\\gamma\\cdot v=\\rho(\\gamma)v\\rho(\\gamma)^{-1}$. In the first-loop-first intrinsic convention, this means forward path transport $T_\\gamma(v)=\\rho(\\gamma)^{-1}v\\rho(\\gamma)$; equivalently the corresponding right action is $v\\cdot\\gamma=\\rho(\\gamma)^{-1}v\\rho(\\gamma)$. Its twisted homology $H_*(E_K;\\operatorname{Ad}\\rho)$ is the intrinsic local-system homology with these transports, equivalently the homology of the cellular local-coefficient complex for a supplied CW structure; the same convention defines boundary-torus twisted homology and the inclusion map $i_*:H_1(\\partial E_K;\\operatorname{Ad}\\rho)\\to H_1(E_K;\\operatorname{Ad}\\rho)$. An **admissible adjoint representation** is one for which there is a nonzero vector $v_\\rho\\in\\mathfrak{sl}_2(\\mathbb C)$ fixed by the peripheral adjoint action such that $\\{[v_\\rho\\otimes\\lambda],[v_\\rho\\otimes\\mu]\\}$ is a basis of $H_1(\\partial E_K;\\operatorname{Ad}\\rho)$ and $\\ker i_*$ is one-dimensional. For an irreducible affine character curve $X$, its function field $\\mathbb C(X)$ is the fraction field of its coordinate domain. A **tautological representation** over $\\mathbb C(X)$ is a homomorphism $\\rho_X:\\pi_1(E_K)\\to SL_2(\\mathbb C(X))$ whose character is the generic character of $X$. A **smooth projective completion** consists of a smooth complete curve $\\overline X$ with a dense open subset $U$ identified with the normalization $X^\\nu$ of $X$, with its finite birational normalization map $\\nu:U\\to X$ recorded. When $X$ is smooth, $\\nu$ identifies $U$ with $X$, recovering the usual completion. An **ideal point** is a point $x\\in\\overline X\\setminus U$; points of $U$ over singular affine points of $X$ are affine branches and are retained inside $U$. Such an ideal point defines the discrete valuation $v_x(f)=\\operatorname{ord}_x(f)$ on $\\mathbb C(X)$. The valuation ring is $\\mathcal O_x=\\{f:v_x(f)\\ge0\\}$, and a **uniformizer** is an element $\\pi\\in\\mathcal O_x$ with $v_x(\\pi)=1$. Its residue field is $k_x=\\mathcal O_x/(\\pi)$. An $\\mathcal O_x$-lattice in $\\mathbb C(X)^2$ is a free rank-two $\\mathcal O_x$-submodule spanning $\\mathbb C(X)^2$; two lattices are homothetic if one is a nonzero scalar multiple of the other. The **Bruhat–Tits tree** has homothety classes of these lattices as vertices, with two vertices adjacent if representatives satisfy $\\pi\\Lambda\\subsetneq\\Lambda'\\subsetneq\\Lambda$ and $\\Lambda/\\Lambda'$ is one-dimensional over $k_x$. Whenever the tautological representation exists, it acts on this tree. The ideal point, valuation, action, and any essential surface produced from the action are its Culler–Shalen data; that surface is called the associated Culler–Shalen surface.",
