          "axiom_use": "Assumes AC through the link-complement manifold and covering-space hypotheses; the mod-two meridian character itself is choice-free.",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r$ be a nonempty finite oriented smooth link in the standard oriented three-sphere, and put $X_L=S^3\\setminus L$. A small meridian about $K_i$ is the boundary of a normal disk fiber; its orientation is immaterial modulo two. The mod-two meridian character is the homomorphism $\\epsilon_L:\\pi_1(X_L)\\to\\mathbb Z/2$ that sends every component meridian to $1$. For each $K_i$, choose an oriented tubular chart, meaning a diffeomorphism from a normal disk bundle onto a neighborhood, written $\\varphi_i:(\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z)\\times D^2\\to N_i$ and respecting the core orientation. Let $\\lambda_i$ be the parallel longitude given by $\\varphi_i((\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z)\\times\\{z_0\\})$ for a fixed $z_0\\in\\partial D^2$, and set $\\eta_i=\\epsilon_L([\\lambda_i])\\in\\{0,1\\}$. The double branched cover $\\Sigma(L)$ is the closed three-manifold obtained by taking the connected two-sheeted cover of $X_L$ classified by $\\ker\\epsilon_L$ and extending it over each $K_i$ with local normal-disk model $(s,z)\\mapsto(s,e^{\\mathrm i\\eta_i s}z^2)$ in these coordinates. Give its total space the orientation for which the covering map is orientation-preserving away from the branch locus."
