      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-heegaard-floer-plus-infinity-absolute-grading",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Heegaard Floer plus and infinity groups and absolute grading for rational homology spheres",
          "deps": [
            "def-given-spin-and-spinc-structures-and-associated-spinor-bundles"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "justified_by": [],
          "statement": "A rational homology sphere is a closed connected oriented three-manifold $Y$ with $H_*(Y;\\mathbb Q)\\cong H_*(S^3;\\mathbb Q)$. Let $\\mathfrak t$ be a Spin$^c$ structure on $Y$, meaning a principal $\\mathrm{Spin}^c(3)$ lift of the oriented frame bundle. A pointed Heegaard diagram for $(Y,\\mathfrak t)$ consists of a closed oriented surface $\\Sigma$ of genus $g$, two systems $\\boldsymbol\\alpha=(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_g)$ and $\\boldsymbol\\beta=(\\beta_1,\\ldots,\\beta_g)$ of pairwise disjoint simple closed curves which bound complete disk systems in the two genus-$g$ handlebodies of a Heegaard splitting of $Y$, with every $\\alpha_i$ transverse to every $\\beta_j$, and a basepoint $z\\in\\Sigma\\setminus(\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cup\\boldsymbol\\beta)$; the diagram is restricted to the generators assigned to $\\mathfrak t$. Compactness of $\\Sigma$ makes the transverse intersection set finite. A periodic domain is an integral two-chain in the complementary regions whose boundary is an integral sum of attaching curves. The diagram is strongly admissible for $\\mathfrak t$ when every nonzero periodic domain $P$ with $n_z(P)=0$ and $\\langle c_1(\\mathfrak t),[P]\\rangle\\ge0$ has at least one positive and one negative region coefficient.\n\nFor such a diagram, put $\\mathbb T_\\alpha=\\alpha_1\\times\\cdots\\times\\alpha_g$ and $\\mathbb T_\\beta=\\beta_1\\times\\cdots\\times\\beta_g$ in $\\operatorname{Sym}^g(\\Sigma)=\\Sigma^g/S_g$. A generator is an intersection point $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb T_\\alpha\\cap\\mathbb T_\\beta$. A Whitney disk class $\\phi\\in\\pi_2(\\mathbf x,\\mathbf y)$ is a relative homotopy class of disks in $\\operatorname{Sym}^g(\\Sigma)$ with boundary arcs on the two product tori and endpoints $\\mathbf x,\\mathbf y$; its Maslov index is $\\mu(\\phi)$ and its local multiplicity at $z$ is $n_z(\\phi)$. For a regular almost-complex datum for which every relevant count is finite and the disk-count operator squares to zero, define $CF^\\infty(Y,\\mathfrak t)$ over $\\mathbb F_2[U,U^{-1}]$ on generators $[\\mathbf x,i]$, $i\\in\\mathbb Z$, with differential\n$$\n\\partial^\\infty[\\mathbf x,i]=\\sum_{\\mathbf y}\\sum_{\\substack{\\phi\\in\\pi_2(\\mathbf x,\\mathbf y)\\\\\\mu(\\phi)=1,\\ n_z(\\phi)\\ge0}}\\#_2\\bigl(\\mathcal M(\\phi)/\\mathbb R\\bigr)[\\mathbf y,i-n_z(\\phi)].\n$$\nHere $\\mathbb F_2$ is the field with two elements, $U[\\mathbf x,i]=[\\mathbf x,i-1]$, and $\\#_2$ is the finite count modulo two. The submodule generated by $[\\mathbf x,i]$ with $i<0$ is $CF^-(Y,\\mathfrak t)$, $CF^+(Y,\\mathfrak t)=CF^\\infty/CF^-$, and $HF^\\infty$ and $HF^+$ denote their homology when the differential is established. Relative Maslov gradings are fixed by the Fredholm index and the shift under $U$ is $-2$.\n\nWhen the standard absolute rational Maslov grading is available, normalize it so that the generator of $HF^\\infty(S^3)$ has degree zero. For a smooth Spin$^c$ cobordism $(W,\\mathfrak s)$ between rational homology spheres, the induced map is assigned degree shift\n$$\n\\frac{\\langle c_1(\\mathfrak s)^2\\rangle-2\\chi(W)-3\\sigma(W)}{4},\n$$\nwhere $c_1(\\mathfrak s)$ is the first Chern class, $\\chi(W)$ is Euler characteristic, $\\sigma(W)$ is the signature, and its square is evaluated using the rational intersection form of $W$. This is a convention for a Floer package when constructed; it asserts neither existence of generic regular data nor that the disk-count differential squares to zero. Heegaard invariance, the absolute grading on general $(Y,\\mathfrak t)$, and cobordism-map properties are separate claims.",
