      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-knot-planar-code",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite planar codes for oriented knot diagrams",
          "deps": [
            "def-oriented-link-in-s-three-and-ambient-isotopy",
            "def-regular-oriented-link-diagram"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "justified_by": [],
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Danny Calegari, Introduction to Knot Theory, §4.2, pp. 34–36",
                "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/notes/knot_theory.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Cole Charbonneau, Computing the Wirtinger Presentation of a Knot Group from a Knot Diagram, §§1.2–3.2",
                "url": "https://site.uvm.edu/cmcharbo/files/2021/12/knot-group-1.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Work with smooth oriented knots in the standard oriented three-sphere; a knot is a one-component oriented link. A **finite planar code for an oriented knot diagram** consists of finite vertex and edge sets, incidence data, a cyclic order of the four half-edges at every crossing vertex, a pairing of opposite half-edges into the two local branches, an orientation of each edge, and a choice of which branch is over at each crossing. Every other vertex is a bend vertex of valence two. The code includes a finite plane-drawing certificate: rational coordinates for the vertices and bends, and a finite polygonal path for every edge. The certificate must realize the incidences and cyclic orders, have no edge self-intersection, and have pairwise disjoint edge interiors except at the specified bend and crossing vertices; at a crossing the two branches must be transverse. These conditions are checked by finitely many exact segment-intersection tests. Edge orientations must join coherently across each paired branch and each bend vertex, and following the edges through the crossing pairings and the degree-two bend vertices must give one oriented circle. Thus an admissible code represents a connected regular oriented diagram of a single knot. If there are no crossing vertices, the code is one oriented polygonal circle and has one arc. Smooth each bend in a small disk disjoint from all crossings and other edges; the resulting smooth diagram has the same crossing data.\n\nAn **under-arc** is a maximal portion of the oriented circle between consecutive points where the knot passes under at a crossing; the over branch is not cut. With no crossings the entire circle is the unique under-arc. A **positive meridian** of an oriented branch is the oriented boundary of a small normal disk, with disk orientation chosen so that the oriented tangent to the branch followed by the disk orientation agrees with the ambient orientation of $S^3$. Planarity, incidence, orientation consistency, one-component tracing and the over/under data are input validity conditions; a procedure receiving an invalid finite record must report invalid input rather than repair it. The code stores no algebraic crossing sign: the oriented plane and the direction of the over branch determine which incident under branch is on its left and which is on its right."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-compact-knot-has-choice-free-tube",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "A compact smooth knot has a choice-free normal tube",
          "deps": [
            "def-normal-addition-map-for-a-euclidean-submanifold",
            "lem-normal-addition-is-a-local-diffeomorphism-along-the-zero-section",
            "thm-lebesgue-number-lemma",
            "def-retraction-and-deformation-retract"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "justified_by": [],
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "John Stillwell, Classical Topology and Combinatorial Group Theory, §4.2.4, Wirtinger presentation proof",
                "url": "https://homepages.math.uic.edu/~kauffman/Stillwell.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Cole Charbonneau, Computing the Wirtinger Presentation of a Knot Group from a Knot Diagram, §2",
                "url": "https://site.uvm.edu/cmcharbo/files/2021/12/knot-group-1.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be the image of a compact smooth embedded circle, and let $E:N^\\perp K\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be the Euclidean normal-addition map $E(p,v)=p+v$. There is a real $\\varepsilon>0$ such that $E$ restricts to a diffeomorphism from $\\{(p,v):\\|v\\|<\\varepsilon\\}$ onto an open neighborhood $N_\\varepsilon$ of $K$. Moreover, $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus K$ strongly deformation retracts onto $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus N_\\varepsilon$ by pushing each nonzero normal vector radially to length $\\varepsilon$. The construction uses only a finite cover of the compact circle and no choice axiom."
        },
        {
