            "def-induced-homomorphism-on-fundamental-groups",
            "thm-induced-fundamental-group-map-functoriality",
            "thm-fundamental-group-of-the-circle",
            "thm-fundamental-group-of-finite-wedge-of-circles",
            "lem-cw-homotopy-equivalence-inclusions-are-strong-deformation-retracts",
            "lem-cw-quotients-and-collapse-of-a-contractible-subcomplex",
            "thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected",
            "thm-seifert-van-kampen",
            "thm-polygonal-jordan-curve"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "John Stillwell, Classical Topology and Combinatorial Group Theory, §4.2.4, Wirtinger presentation proof",
                "url": "https://homepages.math.uic.edu/~kauffman/Stillwell.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Cole Charbonneau, Computing the Wirtinger Presentation of a Knot Group from a Knot Diagram, §2",
                "url": "https://site.uvm.edu/cmcharbo/files/2021/12/knot-group-1.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stefan Friedl, Knot Theory, §5.3, proof of the Wirtinger Presentation Proposition",
                "url": "https://friedl.app.uni-regensburg.de/papers/2024_knot-theory-final.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a smooth oriented knot with a regular diagram represented by a valid finite planar code, and let $c$ be its number of crossings. Choose a sufficiently small closed normal tube $N(K)$ and set $E_K=\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$. If $c>0$, there is a subspace $Y\\subset E_K$, fixed pointwise by a strong deformation retraction of $E_K$, with a finite relative-open cover\n$$Y=U\\cup V_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup V_c.$$\nThe space $U$ deformation retracts to a wedge of $c$ circles. With a common basepoint in the collar above the diagram and access paths in that collar extended into the arc charts, the positive under-arc meridians are its free basis. Each $V_i$ is contractible, $U\\cap V_i$ is an open annulus around the $i$-th crossing attachment, and distinct $V_i$ are disjoint. Thus $V_i$ meets $U$ and any previously attached crossing pieces precisely in $U\\cap V_i$.\n\nFor $c=0$, the Euclidean complement has homotopy type $S^1\\vee S^2$, with the circle represented by the positive meridian. In particular its fundamental group is infinite cyclic; the extra sphere at infinity must not be discarded. In all cases the punctured normal tube retracts radially to its boundary, giving a strong deformation retraction $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus K\\to E_K$. These constructions require only finitely many charts and no choice axiom.",
          "justified_by": []
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-wirtinger-local-meridian-conjugacy",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Local meridian conjugacy at a Wirtinger crossing",
          "deps": [
            "def-knot-planar-code",
            "lem-wirtinger-crossing-ball-cover",
            "def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group",
            "thm-fundamental-group-laws",
            "thm-seifert-van-kampen",
            "thm-group-pushout-as-an-amalgamated-quotient"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Danny Calegari, Introduction to Knot Theory, §4.2, printed pp. 34–36",
                "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/notes/knot_theory.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "John Stillwell, Classical Topology and Combinatorial Group Theory, §4.2.4",
                "url": "https://homepages.math.uic.edu/~kauffman/Stillwell.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stefan Friedl, Knot Theory, §5.3, proof of the Wirtinger Presentation Proposition",
                "url": "https://friedl.app.uni-regensburg.de/papers/2024_knot-theory-final.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Orient the projection plane by the viewing normal and the ambient orientation. At an oriented crossing let $o$ be the over-arc, and let $n_o$ be its positive in-plane normal: the ordered pair consisting of the over-tangent and $n_o$ is positive. Call the under-side from which $n_o$ points the source $s$, and the side toward which it points the target $t$. A positive meridian is the oriented boundary of a small normal disk whose orientation, preceded by the oriented knot tangent, is the ambient orientation. With the common upper-collar basing paths, the based positive meridians satisfy\n$$m_t=m_o^{-1}m_s m_o.$$\nThis side-based equation is independent of the direction of the understrand. In an oriented chart with over-tangent rightward, the target is the upper side $L$ and source the lower side $R$, so equivalently $m_R=m_o m_Lm_o^{-1}$. In incoming/outgoing notation, the outgoing under-meridian is $m_o^{-1}m_{\\rm in}m_o$ if the under-tangent points along $n_o$, and is $m_om_{\\rm in}m_o^{-1}$ otherwise. Reversing a chosen meridian loop inverts its class. Changing every common access path by the same based loop conjugates all three classes simultaneously.",
          "justified_by": []
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-knot-group-infinity-collar",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Removing the point at infinity preserves the knot group",
          "deps": [
            "def-oriented-link-in-s-three-and-ambient-isotopy",
            "lem-wirtinger-crossing-ball-cover",
            "def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group",
            "thm-fundamental-group-laws",
            "def-induced-homomorphism-on-fundamental-groups",
            "thm-induced-fundamental-group-map-functoriality",
            "thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected",
            "def-simply-connected",
            "thm-seifert-van-kampen",
            "cor-seifert-van-kampen-simply-connected-overlap"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Stefan Friedl, Knot Theory, §5.3, proof of the Wirtinger Presentation Proposition",
                "url": "https://friedl.app.uni-regensburg.de/papers/2024_knot-theory-final.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a compact smooth knot and let $b\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus K$. The inclusion $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus K\\hookrightarrow S^3\\setminus K$ induces an isomorphism\n$$\\pi_1(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus K,b)\\xrightarrow{\\ \\cong\\ }\\pi_1(S^3\\setminus K,b).$$",
          "justified_by": []
        },
        {
          "id": "alg-knot-wirtinger-presentation-from-code",
          "kind": "algorithm",
          "title": "Wirtinger presentation from a finite diagram code",
          "deps": [
            "def-knot-planar-code",
            "lem-wirtinger-local-meridian-conjugacy",
            "def-group-presentation"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Cole Charbonneau, Computing the Wirtinger Presentation of a Knot Group from a Knot Diagram, §§2–3",
                "url": "https://site.uvm.edu/cmcharbo/files/2021/12/knot-group-1.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Danny Calegari, Introduction to Knot Theory, §4.2, printed pp. 34–36",
                "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/notes/knot_theory.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "The input is a finite record $D$ and a designated starting under-arc $a_0$ of one oriented knot. First check that the vertex, edge, and segment lists are finite; that every vertex, bend, and point on an edge polyline has rational coordinates; and that each polyline has the prescribed incident endpoints. Before geometric tests, delete consecutive repeated coordinates from each polyline; reject an edge with no positive-length segment or a crossing with a zero-length incident ray. Check that every crossing has four incident half-edges, a recorded cyclic order and a pairing into alternating opposite branches, and one designated over-branch; every other vertex must be a valence-two bend. Check that edge orientations agree at bends and continue coherently through each paired branch, and that following these pairings and bends traces one oriented circle. If $a_0$ is not one of the resulting under-arcs, or any structural check fails, return invalid-input.\n\nFor every unordered pair of line segments, compute the determinant of their rational direction vectors. If it is nonzero, solve the two-coordinate intersection equations over $\\mathbb{Q}$ and check whether the solution lies on both closed segments. If the determinant is zero, test collinearity and overlap by exact rational determinant and endpoint-order comparisons. Reject every positive-length overlap. Allow a singleton intersection only at the common endpoint of consecutive segments in one edge polyline, or at a shared declared bend or crossing vertex with the recorded endpoint incidences. Reject all other intersections, including all other self-intersections. At each crossing, sort the four nonzero incident rays by half-plane and exact determinant comparisons; reject the input if two rays have the same direction, if this geometric cyclic order disagrees with the recorded order, or if the paired branches do not alternate. These checks are finite.\n\nAfter validation, traverse the oriented knot once from $a_0$, label the maximal under-arcs $a_1,\\ldots,a_m$, and introduce one symbol $x_i$ for the positive meridian of each $a_i$. At each crossing, let the outgoing over-half-edge be the ray in the direction of the oriented over-tangent. In the verified counterclockwise cyclic order, the under-half-edge immediately following it is the target side $a_t$; the other under-half-edge is the source side $a_s$. Read the over-arc $a_o$ and append exactly the relator $x_o^{-1}x_sx_ox_t^{-1}$. This rule uses the positive in-plane normal, which points counterclockwise from the oriented over-tangent, and does not depend on the under-strand direction. The orientation of each positive meridian is fixed by requiring the oriented knot tangent followed by its normal-disk orientation to give the ambient orientation. Use the diagram's common upper-collar access paths to base all meridians at one point in that collar. If the chosen complement basepoint lies elsewhere, prepend the same path from it to the collar basepoint to every access path. Interpret $x_i$ as the resulting based positive meridian. Return the finite group presentation with generators $x_1,\\ldots,x_m$ and one relator per crossing.",
          "justified_by": []
        },
