          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r\\subset S^3$ be a nonempty oriented smooth link with $r\\ge1$ components, and let $X_L=S^3\\setminus L$ be its open complement. For each component choose a smooth fibrewise norm on its normal plane bundle $\\nu(K_i)$ and a tubular map $\\Phi_i$ from an open neighborhood of the zero section, fixing the zero section, together with a radius $\\epsilon_i>0$ whose closed disk subbundle lies in that neighborhood. Require the resulting closed tubes $N(K_i)=\\Phi_i(D_{\\epsilon_i}\\nu(K_i))$ to be pairwise disjoint, and put $N(L)=\\bigcup_{i=1}^rN(K_i)$ and $E_L=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}_{S^3}N(L)$, the resulting compact smooth $3$-manifold with boundary. The tube choices are part of the input. Choose a basepoint $b\\in E_L\\subset X_L$. Orient each normal disk so that the positive tangent to $K_i$ followed by its positive normal basis agrees with the fixed orientation of $S^3$. A **positive meridian** $\\mu_i$ of $K_i$ is the oriented boundary of the radius-$\\epsilon_i$ normal disk in one fibre of $\\nu(K_i)$; the curve lies on $\\partial E_L$ and is disjoint from every component of $L$; its spanning normal disk meets $K_i$ transversely once with positive sign and misses every other component. Alexander duality identifies $H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$ with $\\mathbb Z^r$ and identifies the classes of $\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_r$ with its standard basis. Write $G=\\pi_1(X_L,b)$ and let $\\phi:G\\to H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$ be the first Hurewicz homomorphism, equivalently abelianization. The subgroup $\\ker\\phi$ is normal. The connected covering $p:X_L^{\\mathrm{ab}}\\to X_L$ classified by $\\ker\\phi$ is the **maximal abelian cover**; it is regular and its deck group is identified with $G/\\ker\\phi\\cong H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$. Put $\\Lambda_r=\\mathbb Z[H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)]\\cong\\mathbb Z[t_1^{\\pm1},\\ldots,t_r^{\\pm1}]$, where $t_i$ is the deck transformation corresponding to $[\\mu_i]$. The isomorphism sends each variable to its group-ring basis element; monomials $t_1^{n_1}\\cdots t_r^{n_r}$ correspond bijectively to the basis elements indexed by $n_1[\\mu_1]+\\cdots+n_r[\\mu_r]$. Define the absolute multivariable Alexander module by\n\n$$A_L=H_1(X_L^{\\mathrm{ab}};\\mathbb Z),$$\n\nas a left $\\Lambda_r$-module under the deck action. “Absolute” means this is ordinary homology of the covering space, not relative homology with respect to a fibre over the basepoint."
