          "statement": "Let $\\Lambda_2=\\mathbb Z[t_1^{\\pm1},t_2^{\\pm1}]$, $\\Lambda_1=\\mathbb Z[t^{\\pm1}]$, and let $\\eta:\\Lambda_2\\to\\Lambda_1$ send $t_1,t_2$ to $t$. Choose standard pairwise-disjoint radius-$\\epsilon$ tubes with $0<\\epsilon<1/\\sqrt2$. Let $H$ be the standard Hopf link $\\{z=0\\}\\cup\\{w=0\\}$ in $S^3=\\{(z,w)\\in\\mathbb C^2:|z|^2+|w|^2=1\\}$, orienting its components so the increasing phase meridians are positive, and let $U$ be the standard two-component unlink whose components lie in separate $3$-balls. Choose a common basepoint for the two based positive-meridian loops in the unlink exterior and use it for the unlink cover. For $H$, the complement is $T^2\\times(0,1)$ and the compact exterior is $T^2\\times I$; its maximal abelian cover is $\\mathbb R^2\\times I$, so its absolute multivariable Alexander module is $A_H=0$. Its total-linking cyclic cover is $S^1\\times\\mathbb R\\times I$, with trivial $t$-action on first homology, so the one-variable Alexander module is $\\Lambda_1/(t-1)$, whereas $A_H\\otimes_{\\Lambda_2,\\eta}\\Lambda_1=0$. For $U$, the compact exterior has free fundamental group on its two positive meridians. The meridian map from $S^1\\vee S^1$ induces an isomorphism on first homology of the corresponding maximal abelian covers, whose graph-cover chain complex is $\\Lambda_2^2\\xrightarrow{(t_1-1,\\ t_2-1)}\\Lambda_2$; its first homology is free of rank one, so $A_U\\cong\\Lambda_2$. The total-linking cyclic graph-cover complex computing its first homology is $\\Lambda_1^2\\xrightarrow{(t-1,\\ t-1)}\\Lambda_1$, with first homology $\\Lambda_1$. These computations distinguish chain specialization from tensoring the multivariable homology module.\n\nThe projection below is schematic: the upper crossing has the first component $K_1$ (blue) over, and the lower crossing has the second component $K_2$ (red) over. The calculations use the stated coordinate and meridian graph-cover homology models, not the drawing.\n\