          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $J=J_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup J_n$ be an oriented link of $n\\ge1$ smooth components in $S^3$. Choose pairwise disjoint closed tubular neighborhoods $N(J_i)$, let $X=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}(\\bigcup_iN(J_i))$, and let $\\mu_i$ be the positive meridian of $J_i$. Let $\\lambda_i$ be the longitude parallel to $J_i$ with zero self-linking. Alexander duality identifies $H_1(X;\\mathbb Z)$ with $\\mathbb Z^n$ on the meridian basis $[\\mu_1],\\ldots,[\\mu_n]$; for $i\\ne j$, define $\\operatorname{lk}_{S^3}(J_i,J_j)$ to be the $j$-th coordinate of $[\\lambda_i]$, the usual oriented linking number. Use the boundary-torus intersection convention $\\mu_i\\mathbin{\\cdot}\\lambda_i=+1$. Give $J_i$ an integer framing $f_i$ and fill along $\\delta_i=\\lambda_i+f_i\\mu_i$. Let $A$ be the symmetric integer matrix with $A_{ii}=f_i$ and $A_{ij}=\\operatorname{lk}_{S^3}(J_i,J_j)$ for $i\\ne j$. Assume $\\det A\\ne0$, and let $Y$ be the resulting oriented surgery manifold, oriented as the boundary of the 4-dimensional surgery handlebody with outward normal first. Then $H_1(Y;\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\mathbb Z^n/A\\mathbb Z^n$. For torsion classes $[C],[D]\\in H_1(Y;\\mathbb Z)$ represented by oriented 1-cycles, choose $m>0$ and an integral 2-chain $F$ with $\\partial F=mC$ and define $\\lambda_Y([C],[D])=(F\\mathbin{\\cdot}D)/m\\pmod{\\mathbb Z}$, where the intersection is signed by the orientation of $Y$. If $x,y\\in\\mathbb Z^n$ are meridian-coordinate representatives of $[C],[D]$, then\n$$\n\\lambda_Y([x],[y])=-x^TA^{-1}y\\pmod{\\mathbb Z}.\n$$",
