          "statement": "Let $F$ be an oriented smooth surface and let $c:S^1\\hookrightarrow F$ be a smooth embedded circle. Choose an annular coordinate map $\\phi:S^1\\times[-1,1]\\to A\\subset F$ whose product orientation agrees with the orientation of $F$ and whose core $\\phi(S^1\\times\\{0\\})$ is $c(S^1)$. Choose a smooth function $\\rho:[-1,1]\\to[0,1]$ that is $0$ near $-1$ and $1$ near $1$. The positive **Dehn twist about $c$ in the chosen annular coordinates** is the diffeomorphism that is the identity outside $A$ and on $A$ is\n$$\n\\phi(e^{i\\theta},t)\\longmapsto\\phi(e^{i(\\theta+2\\pi\\rho(t))},t).\n$$\nThe negative Dehn twist is its inverse. The annular coordinates and cutoff are part of the data whenever a representative diffeomorphism is used; its mapping class is its class under smooth isotopy through orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms of $F$."
