            ]
          },
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Identify the standard oriented three-sphere with $S^3=\\{(z_1,z_2)\\in\\mathbb C^2:|z_1|^2+|z_2|^2=1\\}$ and choose $0<r<1$. The standard unknotted Heegaard torus is $T_r=\\{|z_1|=r,\\ |z_2|=\\sqrt{1-r^2}\\}$; its two sides are solid tori. In the angular coordinates $(u,v)\\in(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^2$ given by $(u,v)\\mapsto(r e^{2\\pi i u},\\sqrt{1-r^2}e^{2\\pi i v})$, let $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ be the first and second coordinate classes in $H_1(T_r;\\mathbb Z)$. For coprime nonzero integers $p,q$, the **oriented torus knot** $T(p,q)$ is the image of $s\\mapsto(r e^{2\\pi i p s},\\sqrt{1-r^2}e^{2\\pi i q s})$, for $s\\in\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$, oriented by increasing $s$; it represents the primitive slope $p\\alpha+q\\beta$. The mirror convention is the reflection $(z_1,z_2)\\mapsto(z_1,\\overline{z_2})$, which sends $T(p,q)$ to $T(p,-q)$ with image orientation, and reversing the knot orientation gives $T(-p,-q)$. For nonzero $p,q$ with $d=\\gcd(|p|,|q|)>1$, the corresponding torus link is the union of $d$ disjoint parallel copies of the primitive slope $(p/d,q/d)$; it is not a single embedded curve of class $p\\alpha+q\\beta$. The exterior is $E_K=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ for a chosen closed tubular neighborhood. Orient each normal disk so that a positive tangent vector to $K$ followed by a positive basis of the disk gives the orientation of $S^3$. A **positive meridian** is the boundary of such a disk with its induced boundary orientation. Let $\\pi:\\partial E_K\\to K$ be the projection of the chosen normal-disk tube. A **preferred longitude** is an oriented simple closed curve $\\lambda\\subset\\partial E_K$ for which $\\pi|_\\lambda$ has degree $+1$ and $i_*[\\lambda]=0$ in $H_1(E_K;\\mathbb Z)$, where $i:\\partial E_K\\hookrightarrow E_K$ is inclusion; this definition alone asserts neither existence nor uniqueness."
