          "statement": "Let $V=S^1\\times D^2$ be the standard oriented solid torus, with oriented core $S^1\\times\\{0\\}$. A **pattern** is a smooth oriented knot $P$ contained in the interior of $V$, together with a chosen closed tubular neighbourhood $N(P)$ contained in the interior of $V$. Its **winding number** $w(P)$ is the integer by which the homology class $[P]$ maps to $H_1(V;\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\mathbb Z$ under the isomorphism that sends the oriented core to $1$. The **pattern exterior** is $X_P=V\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(P)$; its **outer torus** is $\\partial V$ and its **inner torus** is $\\partial N(P)$. A **companion** is a smooth oriented knot $K\\subset S^3$ with a specified orientation-preserving tubular embedding $e:V\\to S^3$ that sends the core to $K$ and identifies the solid-torus meridian and product longitude with the positive meridian and preferred longitude of $K$. The **satellite knot** $P(K)$ is $e(P)$, with chosen exterior $E_{P(K)}=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}e(N(P))$; the image $e(\\partial V)$ is its **companion torus**. The outer torus is **incompressible** when it bounds no properly embedded disk in $X_P$ with essential boundary on $\\partial V$. It is **product-parallel** to the inner torus when $X_P$ is a product $T^2\\times[0,1]$ with those tori as its ends. This definition alone asserts neither incompressibility nor essentiality of the companion torus."
