      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-disk-like-capped-grope-height-one-and-one-half",
          "kind": "definition",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "title": "Disk-like capped gropes of height one and one-half",
          "justified_by": [],
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Stefan Behrens et al., The Disc Embedding Theorem",
                "url": "https://dokumen.pub/the-disc-embedding-theorem-0198841310-9780198841319.html",
                "locator": "Definitions 12.3, 12.5–12.6, and 12.10–12.12, printed pp.173–181"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Use the framed four-dimensional block and stage conventions for gropes in the cited definitions. A **capped grope** is a staged thickening whose bottom surface stage is joined along curves in a symplectic basis to higher surface stages, with the final tip circles capped by framed immersed disks. A **disk-like capped grope** has one connected bottom surface stage with exactly one attaching circle. Its **height** is the number of successive surface stages on each side of the symplectic basis, with caps terminating the stages. A disk-like capped grope has **height one and one-half**, written $1.5$, when its two sides have heights $(1,0)$ or $(0,1)$ in the asymmetric convention; equivalently, one half of the first-stage basis has one additional surface stage and the other half is capped immediately. The **attaching region** is the framed solid-torus neighborhood of the single attaching circle. For an immersed disk, a **double-point loop** is the image of a path in the domain disk joining the two preimages of a transverse self-intersection. In the disk-embedding property for an input capped grope, this loop is read as the corresponding element of the grope's fundamental group; choosing a basing path from the grope basepoint fixes that based element."
        },
        {
          "id": "def-freedman-good-group-and-disk-embedding-data",
          "kind": "definition",
          "deps": [
            "def-locally-flat-proper-surface-and-concordance-collars"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "title": "Freedman disk-embedding data",
          "justified_by": [],
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Michael H. Freedman and Frank Quinn, Topology of 4-Manifolds",
                "url": "https://archive.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/4789/1/FreedmanQuinn-TopologyOf4Manifolds-PMS39.pdf",
                "locator": "§1.7, intersection and self-intersection numbers, printed pp.20–23; §5.1, Theorems 5.1A–5.1B, printed pp.85–87"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stefan Behrens et al., The Disc Embedding Theorem",
                "url": "https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780198841319.001.0001",
                "locator": "Chapter 11, intersection numbers and the relative disc-embedding statement"
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark Powell, Arunima Ray and Peter Teichner, The 4-dimensional disc embedding theorem and dual spheres",
                "url": "https://www.maths.gla.ac.uk/~mpowell/s00029-025-01069-y.pdf",
                "locator": "Selecta Mathematica 31:80 (2025), Theorem A, PDF p.3; Section 3, PDF p.13 (extending Whitney framing); Remark 6.3, PDF pp.22–23 (prescribed boundary framing and its relative extension obstruction)"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $X$ be a compact connected oriented topological $4$-manifold with nonempty boundary, let $x_0\\in X$, and put $G=\\pi_1(X,x_0)$. A **finite relative disk-embedding datum** consists of proper generic immersions $f_i:(D^2,S^1)\\looparrowright(X,\\partial X)$, $1\\le i\\le n$, whose boundary circles are embedded and pairwise disjoint, together with framed generic immersions $g_i:S^2\\looparrowright\\operatorname{int}X$ that are algebraically dual to the disks. A topological generic immersion has the local models of a smooth generic immersion: at a point with one inverse image its image sheet is locally flat, with a proper half-space chart at boundary points in which the disk is a half-plane and its boundary lies on the ambient boundary, and each of its finitely many self-double points has exactly two preimages and the coordinate-plane model $\\mathbb R^2\\times\\{0\\}$ and $\\{0\\}\\times\\mathbb R^2$ in a chart of $X$. All mutual intersections among the disk and sphere maps are also required to be finite and transverse, so the equivariant intersection sums below are finite. Proper means that the inverse image of $\\partial X$ is exactly the domain boundary. Each disk $f_i$ is supplied with a specified normal framing along $S^1$, equivalently the framing of its boundary attaching solid torus; this boundary framing is part of the datum and need not extend over $D^2$. Each sphere $g_i$ is supplied with a trivialization of its normal microbundle over $S^2$. Choose paths from $x_0$ to the immersed surfaces and orient the surfaces. Each transverse intersection $p$ of two surfaces contributes $\\epsilon(p)g_p$ to $\\lambda$, where $\\epsilon(p)\\in\\{1,-1\\}$ compares their induced local orientation with the chosen orientation of $X$, and $g_p\\in G$ is the loop that travels along the chosen path to the first surface, then to $p$, and returns along the second surface and its chosen path. Thus $\\lambda$ is a signed equivariant intersection sum in $\\mathbb Z[G]$. Wall's self-intersection sum $\\mu$ is the signed sum over self-double points in $\\mathbb Z[G]/\\langle g-g^{-1}:g\\in G\\rangle$, because interchanging the two sheets replaces a label by its inverse. The relative normal Euler (boundary-framing) obstruction of $f_i$ is zero precisely when its specified boundary framing extends over $D^2$; the datum does not assume that it vanishes. The spheres are **algebraically dual** when $\\lambda(f_i,g_j)=\\delta_{ij}$, $\\lambda(g_i,g_j)=0$ for every $i,j$, and $\\mu(g_i)=0$ for every $i$. The disk data are **algebraically unobstructed** when also $\\lambda(f_i,f_j)=0$ for $i\\ne j$ and $\\mu(f_i)=0$ for every $i$."
