          "statement": "Let $T$ be a compact strict Morse tangle in $\\mathbb C\\times[a,b]$ whose skeleton is one oriented arc with exactly one boundary point on each boundary plane, oriented from the lower point to the upper point. For sufficiently large $R$, close $T$ to an embedded oriented circle $K_R$ by adding a lower cap with one nondegenerate minimum, an upper cap with one nondegenerate maximum, and a return arc that is vertical at horizontal coordinate $R$ over $[a,b]$. Choose the smooth joins in regular height collars so that varying $R$ moves only regular arcs and fixes both cap critical neighborhoods. Let $\\operatorname{cl}$ be the linear map that closes an interval chord diagram on $T$ to a circle chord diagram by joining its two boundary ends along the outside closure arc. For closed chord diagrams, let $\\#$ be connected-sum multiplication, formed by concatenating diagrams at the closure cut. For oriented knot closures, connected sum is formed by deleting a short arc from each knot and joining the four ends in the orientation-preserving order outside their compact tangles. Then\n$$\nZ(K_R)=\\operatorname{cl}(Z(T)),\n$$\nindependently of the sufficiently large choice of $R$. If $T_1,T_2$ each have one-arc skeletons and are compact strict Morse one-in one-out tangles whose vertical composite is smooth and embedded, then the closure of $T_2\\circ T_1$ is the connected sum of their closures and\n$$\nZ(\\operatorname{cl}(T_2\\circ T_1))=Z(\\operatorname{cl}(T_2))\\# Z(\\operatorname{cl}(T_1)).\n$$\nThe figure is schematic: height increases upward, arrows give the orientation, the lower and upper caps have one minimum and one maximum, and the return arc is the vertical branch at $R$. The second panel shows the oriented seam used for vertical stacking and closure; neither panel is used as a proof.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8,thick]\n  \\begin{scope}\n    \\draw[->] (-0.8,0.1)--(-0.8,3.25) node[above] {$t$};\n    \\draw[very thick,->] (0,0.65