          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "statement": "Let $A$ be an indeterminate, put $q=A^4$, $q_K=A^2$, and $q_L=A^{-2}=q_K^{-1}$, and work over $F=\\mathbb Q(A)$. An oriented knot in $S^3$ means a smooth embedded circle with an orientation, considered up to orientation-preserving ambient isotopy. For an integer $r$, put\n$$[r]=\\frac{q_K^r-q_K^{-r}}{q_K-q_K^{-1}},\\qquad [m]!=\\prod_{r=1}^{m}[r]\\quad(m\\ge1),\\qquad [0]!=1.$$\nFor each $N\\ge1$, put $q_N=\\exp(2\\pi i/N)$.\nFor each integer $N\\ge1$, let $V_N$ have basis $v_0,\\ldots,v_{N-1}$ and define the $U_{q_L}(\\mathfrak{sl}_2)$ action in the library coproduct convention by\n$$K_Lv_j=q_L^{N-1-2j}v_j,\\qquad Ev_j=[N-j]v_{j-1},\\qquad Fv_j=[j+1]v_{j+1},$$\nwhere $v_{-1}=v_N=0$. In the universal $R$-matrix formula use $q_K$ and $H v_j=(N-1-2j)v_j$; on $V_N\\otimes V_N$ its action is the finite sum\n$$\\mathcal R_N=q_K^{H\\otimes H/2}\\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}q_K^{r(r-1)/2}\\frac{(q_K-q_K^{-1})^r}{[r]!}\\,E^r\\otimes F^r,$$\nand the positive crossing is $c_N=P\\mathcal R_N$, where $P(x\\otimes y)=y\\otimes x$. This is an irreducible module of dimension $N$. Equip these matrices with the Kauffman-bracket half-ribbon data: the twist matrix is the scalar $\\theta_N=\\lambda_N I$ with $\\lambda_N=(-1)^{N-1}A^{N^2-1}$, and the matrix $G_N=(-1)^{N-1}\\operatorname{diag}(A^{2(N-1-2j)})_{j=0}^{N-1}$ is the pivotal comparison used in the closure trace. The resulting generic quantum dimension is\n$$d_N=\\operatorname{Tr}(G_N)=(-1)^{N-1}\\frac{A^{2N}-A^{-2N}}{A^2-A^{-2}}.$$\nUse the contragredient left-dual action $(x\\cdot f)(v)=f(S(x)v)$ on $V_N^*$, with evaluation $\\operatorname{ev}(f\\otimes v)=f(v)$ and coevaluation $1\\mapsto\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}v_j\\otimes v_j^*$. The ambient category is the $F$-linear category generated by the modules $V_N$ for all $N\\ge1$ under finite tensor products, contragredient duals, finite direct sums and images of module-idempotents, whose morphisms are module homomorphisms, and whose tensor-product action is induced by the stated Hopf coproduct; its tensor unit is $F$ with the trivial action, so its tensor-unit endomorphism ring is $F$. These matrix data satisfy the ribbon-category axioms: braiding and inverse braiding, both duality snakes, monoidal pivotal comparison, balancing and dual compatibility of twist. Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice for the framed-tangle and Markov constructions. An oriented knot $K\\subset S^3$ presented as the closure of an $n$-strand braid $\\beta$ has generic normalized value\n$$J_N^{\\mathrm{gen}}(K;q)=\\lambda_N^{-w(\\beta)}\\frac{\\operatorname{Tr}(G_N^{\\otimes n}\\rho_N(\\beta))}{d_N}\\in F,$$\nwhere, for a word $\\beta=\\sigma_{i_1}^{\\varepsilon_1}\\cdots\\sigma_{i_m}^{\\varepsilon_m}$ with $\\varepsilon_j\\in\\{1,-1\\}$, $w(\\beta):=\\sum_{j=1}^m\\varepsilon_j$, and $\\rho_N(\\beta)$ is obtained by replacing each positive braid generator by $c_N$ on the corresponding adjacent tensor factors and each inverse generator by $c_N^{-1}$, then composing in word order. This quotient is an integral Laurent polynomial in $A^4$. Define the **normalized colored Jones sequence** by the unique $J_N(K;q)\\in\\mathbb Z[q,q^{-1}]$ satisfying $J_N(K;A^4)=J_N^{\\mathrm{gen}}(K;A^4)$. Its root value $J_N(K;q_N)$ is obtained by substituting into this Laurent polynomial. The variable transport to the ordinary Jones convention is $t=q^{-1}=A^{-4}$.",
