              {
                "title": "Krzysztof K. Putyra, A 2-category of chronological cobordisms and odd Khovanov homology, arXiv:1310.1895",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1310.1895",
                "locator": "§§2–3, chronological cobordisms and edge assignments"
              },
              {
                "title": "Nathan Dunfield, Robert Lipshitz, and Dirk Schütz, Local equivalence and refinements of Rasmussen's s-invariant, arXiv:2312.09114v3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.09114v3",
                "locator": "§§2.1–3.1, printed pp. 4–15"
              }
            ]
          },
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "justified_by": [],
          "aliases": [],
          "statement": "Let $D$ be a finite regular oriented link diagram with at least one component, ordered crossings $c_1<\\cdots<c_n$, and a marked point $p$ on the projected link away from crossings. Put $\\mathbb A=\\mathbb Z[X]/(X^2)$ and $\\mathbb F_2=\\mathbb Z/2\\mathbb Z$. At a resolution state $v\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$, let $V_v$ be the free abelian group generated by the resolution circles, order its circle generators, and use the exterior algebra $\\Lambda^*V_v$ as its state module. A wedge of $r$ distinct circle generators has circle quantum degree $k(v)-2r$, where $k(v)=\\operatorname{rank}V_v$; its total normalized degrees are $i(v)=|v|-n_-$ and $q=|v|+n_+-2n_-+k(v)-2r$. For a merge saddle joining circles $C_1,C_2$, let $p:V_v\\to V_{v+e}$ be the quotient sending both to the merged circle and fixing the other generators; the edge map is $\\Lambda^*p$. For a split saddle replacing $C$ by $C_1,C_2$, let $j:V_v\\to V_{v+e}$ send $C$ to $C_1$ and fix the other generators; the unsigned edge map is $z\\mapsto(C_1-C_2)\\wedge\\Lambda^*j(z)$. Choosing $C_2$ instead of $C_1$ gives the same map, while reversing the order of $C_1,C_2$ changes only its sign. Multiply each edge map by a sign $\\epsilon(e)\\in\\{1,-1\\}$, incorporating any circle-generator reordering sign. The direct sum of these state modules with the resulting cube differential is the odd Khovanov cochain complex $C_o(D)$. Write $Z_p$ for the circle containing the mark. Use the right action $z\\cdot X=z\\wedge Z_p$, making $C_o(D)$ a complex of right $\\mathbb A$-modules; since $\\mathbb A$ is commutative these are also ordinary modules over that ring. The action has quantum degree $-2$.\n\nThe square condition is that the sum of the two signed saddle composites around each two-dimensional cube face is zero. An edge sign function satisfying it is called square-compatible; existence and invariance require separate proofs.\n\nLet $\\mathbb R=\\mathbb Z[h,X]/(X^2-hX)$, with $\\deg_q(h)=\\deg_q(X)=-2$. Its Frobenius structure has unit $\\eta(1)=1$, counit $\\epsilon(1)=0,\\epsilon(X)=1$, quotient-ring multiplication, and comultiplication\n$$ \\Delta(1)=1\\otimes X+X\\otimes1-h\\,1\\otimes1,\\qquad \\Delta(X)=X\\otimes X. $$\nApplying these operations to the same normalized cube gives the Bar–Natan cochain complex $C_h(D)$, with circle labels $1,X$ of quantum degrees $1,-1$ and the same normalized state shifts; it is a complex of free $\\mathbb R$-modules, and the marked circle acts by multiplication by $X$.\n\nPut $\\mathbb B=\\mathbb F_2[X]/(X^2)=\\mathbb R/(2,h)$. Modulo $2$, the odd exterior basis vector $C_{i_1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge C_{i_r}$ corresponds to the even tensor state labeling the selected circles $C_{i_j}$ by $X$ and all other circles by $1$. This statewise bijection defines the candidate comparison $f_D:C_o(D)\\otimes\\mathbb F_2\\to C_h(D)\\otimes_{\\mathbb R}\\mathbb B$ after setting $h=0$.\n\nA **local even–odd (LEO) triple** is a triple $(C,D,f)$ consisting of a finitely generated bigraded cochain complex $C$ free over $\\mathbb A$, a finitely generated bigraded cochain complex $D$ free over $\\mathbb R$, and a bigraded $\\mathbb F_2[X]/(X^2)$-linear chain homotopy equivalence\n$$ f:C\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb F_2\\longrightarrow D\\otimes_{\\mathbb R}\\mathbb B, \\qquad \\mathbb B=\\mathbb R/(2,h)=\\mathbb F_2[X]/(X^2), $$\nsuch that $D[h^{-1}]$ is chain homotopy equivalent over $\\mathbb R[h^{-1}]$ to one free rank-one module supported in cohomological degree $0$ and odd quantum degree. For a based knot diagram the intended candidate is $(C_o(D),C_h(D),f_D)$, where $f_D$ identifies the reduced edge maps modulo $2$ and $h$; its satisfaction of the homotopy and localization conditions is a separate theorem.\n\nA local map $(C,D,f)\\to(C',D',f')$ is a pair of bigraded module chain maps $\\alpha:C\\to C'$ and $\\beta:D\\to D'$ such that $\\beta[h^{-1}]$ is a chain homotopy equivalence and the square induced by $f,f',\\alpha,\\beta$ after quotienting by $(2,h)$ commutes up to an $\\mathbb F_2[X]/(X^2)$-linear chain homotopy. Two LEO triples are locally equivalent when there is a local map in each direction."
