          "statement": "Assume countable choice. Let $K_0,K_1\\subset S^3$ be smooth oriented knots. A smooth oriented knot cobordism from $K_0$ to $K_1$ is a compact smooth properly embedded oriented surface $\\Sigma\\subset S^3\\times[0,1]$ whose boundary is $(-K_0\\times\\{0\\})\\sqcup(K_1\\times\\{1\\})$, with product collars at both ends. A **generic movie** for $\\Sigma$ is obtained by first choosing an ambient isotopy, fixed on smaller prescribed end collars, carrying $\\Sigma$ to a surface $\\Sigma'$ on which the ambient time coordinate $h=\\operatorname{pr}_{[0,1]}|_{\\Sigma'}$ is Morse with distinct critical values. Choose a common spatial chart $S^3\\setminus\\{p\\}\\cong\\mathbb R^3$ containing the spatial image of $\\Sigma'$ and a generic planar projection in that chart, retaining the time coordinate and the over/under crossing information, and record the oriented link diagrams of the regular slices $\\Sigma'\\cap(S^3\\times\\{t\\})$. Intermediate regular levels may be links with any finite number of components. The isotopy and projection are chosen so that the finitely many exceptional levels are isolated and distinct: between them the diagrams change by planar isotopy. At each exceptional level there is either one oriented Reidemeister move, at which the surface height is regular, or one nondegenerate surface-height critical point: a minimum (a birth), a maximum (a death), or an index-one critical point (a saddle band move). The movie includes its ordered list of frames and these elementary changes, with its oriented smooth embedded trace ambient isotopic to $\\Sigma$ relative to the retained end collars and boundary parametrizations. No claim that two movies of the same cobordism are equivalent is part of this definition.",
