        {
          "id": "def-sutured-floer-chain-complex",
          "kind": "definition",
          "deps": [
            "def-balanced-taut-sutured-manifold-and-product-region"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "title": "Sutured Floer chain complexes and homology notation",
          "justified_by": [
            "lem-ni-holomorphic-disk-and-jsj-analytic-inputs"
          ],
          "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Andras Juhasz, Floer homology and surface decompositions, arXiv:math/0609779v3, §§2–3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0609779v3",
                "locator": "Printed pp.3–11; balanced sutured diagrams, admissibility, the chain complex, and relative Spin^c structures"
              },
              {
                "title": "Yi Ni, Knot Floer homology detects fibred knots, arXiv:math/0607156v4, §2.2",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0607156v4",
                "locator": "Printed pp.5–7; the sutured Floer invariant associated to a sutured manifold and its diagram-independence construction"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "For a balanced sutured manifold $(M,\\gamma)$, a **balanced sutured Heegaard diagram** is a tuple $\\mathcal H=(\\Sigma,\\boldsymbol\\alpha,\\boldsymbol\\beta)$ where $\\Sigma$ is a compact oriented surface with boundary and each of $\\boldsymbol\\alpha,\\boldsymbol\\beta$ is a finite family of pairwise disjoint simple closed curves in $\\operatorname{int}\\Sigma$. It defines $M$ by attaching $3$-dimensional $2$-handles to $\\Sigma\\times[0,1]$ along $\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\times\\{0\\}$ and $\\boldsymbol\\beta\\times\\{1\\}$, with sutures $\\partial\\Sigma\\times\\{1/2\\}$ on $\\gamma=\\partial\\Sigma\\times[0,1]$. The diagram is balanced when the two families have equal cardinality $d$ and the natural maps from components of $\\partial\\Sigma$ to components of $\\Sigma\\setminus\\bigcup\\boldsymbol\\alpha$ and $\\Sigma\\setminus\\bigcup\\boldsymbol\\beta$ are both surjective. A **domain** is an integral two-chain generated by the closures of regions of $\\Sigma\\setminus(\\bigcup\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cup\\bigcup\\boldsymbol\\beta)$ disjoint from $\\partial\\Sigma$. A **periodic domain** is a domain whose boundary is an integral sum of full $\\alpha$- and $\\beta$-curves. The diagram is **admissible** when each nonzero periodic domain has at least one positive and one negative region coefficient.\n\nThe symmetric product $\\operatorname{Sym}^d(\\Sigma)=\\Sigma^d/S_d$ is the space of unordered $d$-tuples of points. The product tori $\\mathbb T_\\alpha,\\mathbb T_\\beta$ are the images of the products of the $d$ attaching curves in this symmetric product. Require curves from different families to intersect transversely. A **generator** is a point of $\\mathbb T_\\alpha\\cap\\mathbb T_\\beta$, equivalently a collection of $d$ transverse intersection points, one on each attaching curve in each family. A **Whitney disk class** from generator $\\mathbf{x}$ to generator $\\mathbf{y}$ is a relative homotopy class of maps of a disk into $\\operatorname{Sym}^d(\\Sigma)$ whose two boundary arcs lie on the product tori and whose endpoints are $\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y}$; $\\pi_2(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})$ denotes the set of these classes. For $d>0$, fix a smooth path $J_t$ of almost complex structures on $\\operatorname{Sym}^d(\\Sigma)$ for which the product tori are totally real and which is regular for the disk classes used in the differential and its squared-differential argument. Here an almost complex structure is a smooth bundle endomorphism $J$ with $J^2=-\\operatorname{id}$, and regularity means that the linearized Cauchy--Riemann operator at every nonconstant holomorphic strip in those classes is surjective; in particular impose this for Maslov indices one and two. The counts below use such regular data, obtained by generic perturbation within the sutured Floer analytic setup. A representative of a Whitney disk class is a smooth strip $u:\\mathbb R\\times[0,1]\\to\\operatorname{Sym}^d(\\Sigma)$ satisfying $\\partial_su+J_t(u)\\partial_tu=0$, with $u(s,0)\\in\\mathbb T_\\alpha$, $u(s,1)\\in\\mathbb T_\\beta$, and limits $\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y}$ as $s\\to-\\infty,+\\infty$. Its Maslov index is the Fredholm index of the linearized Cauchy--Riemann operator at a representative, and $\\mathcal M_J(\\phi)$ denotes the space of representatives of class $\\phi$. Translation of the real coordinate gives the $\\mathbb R$ action on this moduli space.\n\nWhen $d=0$, $\\operatorname{Sym}^0(\\Sigma)$ is a point, both product tori are that point, and the unique generator is the empty collection.\n\nFor a fixed admissible diagram and the chosen regular data, the **sutured Floer disk-count module** $SFC(\\mathcal H;R)$ is the free $R$-module on its generators, where $R=\\mathbb F_2$ or $\\mathbb Z$. For $R=\\mathbb F_2$, its formal differential counts translation classes modulo two:\n$$\\partial\\mathbf{x}=\\sum_{\\mathbf{y}}\\sum_{\\substack{\\phi\\in\\pi_2(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})\\\\\\mu(\\phi)=1}}\\#_2\\bigl(\\mathcal M_J(\\phi)/\\mathbb R\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf{y}.$$\nFor $R=\\mathbb Z$, use a coherent orientation system, which assigns orientations to determinant lines of the linearized disk operators compatibly with gluing, and replace $\\#_2$ by the signed count $\\#_{\\mathfrak o}$. The homology of this diagram-level differential is denoted $H_*(SFC(\\mathcal H;R))$ once the moduli counts are finite and the differential squares to zero. When $d=0$ the unique empty generator has zero differential. Once admissible diagram-change maps are proved to be chain homotopy equivalences, their common homology is denoted $SFH(M,\\gamma;R)$. This definition fixes the chain-level and coefficient conventions; it does not assert analytic compactness, gluing, invariance, or surface-decomposition results."
        },
