          "id": "def-mirror-of-a-classical-knot",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Mirror of a classical knot",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Fix the orientation-reversing involution $r:S^3\\to S^3$ given by reflection across a fixed equatorial two-sphere containing a chosen standard unknot $U$. For a smooth knot $K\\subset S^3$, its mirror is $\\overline K:=r(K)$, with the orientation transported by $r$. A diagram of the mirror can be obtained by keeping the projected shadow fixed and interchanging the over- and under-crossing data at every crossing; equivalently, one may reflect the planar shadow while retaining the crossing data. These are alternative mirror conventions, not operations to perform together. Since $r^2=\\mathrm{id}$ and $r(U)=U$, mirroring twice returns $K$, and $K$ is the unknot exactly when $\\overline K$ is.",
