          "statement": "Assume full AC. Let $G=\\pi_1(E_K)$ be the group of a smooth oriented knot exterior, with positive meridian $\\mu$ and preferred longitude $\\lambda$. For a finite presentation $G=\\langle g_1,\\ldots,g_n\\mid r_1,\\ldots,r_m\\rangle$, the **$SL_2(\\mathbb C)$ representation variety** is the algebraic set of tuples $(A_1,\\ldots,A_n)\\in SL_2(\\mathbb C)^n$ for which every relator matrix $r_j(A_1,\\ldots,A_n)$ equals the identity. The group $SL_2(\\mathbb C)$ acts on this set by simultaneous conjugation. Its affine categorical quotient, whose coordinate ring is the invariant ring of regular functions, is the **$SL_2(\\mathbb C)$ character variety** $X(E_K)$. For a representation $\\rho$, its character is the function $\\gamma\\mapsto\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho(\\gamma))$. A representation is **reducible** if its matrices preserve a common one-dimensional subspace of $\\mathbb C^2$; otherwise it is irreducible. The peripheral torus group is $\\pi_1(T_K)\\cong\\mathbb Z^2$, generated by the meridian and preferred longitude; its image in $G$ is generated by their commuting classes and need not be isomorphic to $\\mathbb Z^2$. Their image under any representation is a commuting pair of matrices, so it has a common invariant line and is simultaneously triangularizable over $\\mathbb C$. Choosing a common invariant line gives meridian and longitude eigenvalues $(M,L)\\in(\\mathbb C^*)^2$; the unordered boundary character identifies $(M,L)$ with $(M^{-1},L^{-1})$. Simultaneous diagonalizability is stronger than simultaneous triangularizability.",
