          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $D$ be a finite regular oriented link diagram with $c$ crossings, $m\\ge1$ components, and $a$ arcs as defined in the encoded diagram size definition. For a two-component diagram, after one constant-size component-count check, the linking-number scan visits exactly the $c$ crossings, makes at most $c$ component/sign tests and $c$ integer additions, and returns the exact integer half-sum of mixed crossing signs; its absolute value is at most $c/2$ and it is invariant under ambient isotopy of the oriented link.\n\nFor any such diagram and any odd prime $p$, the Fox coloring system has one row per crossing and one column per arc over $\\mathbb F_p$. Matrix construction uses at most $2c$ field additions to combine repeated arc coefficients and $ca$ zero-entry writes. If row reduction uses $r$ pivots and has $f=a-r$ free variables, then $r\\le\\min(c,a)$, pivot search uses at most $ca$ entry tests, and row reduction uses at most $ra+2r(c-1)a$ field arithmetic operations. The algorithm returns exactly $p^f$ Fox colorings. Listing the full solution set after reduction uses at most $p^f(2f(a-f)+a)$ field arithmetic and output-write operations. If only the count is required, $p^f$ can instead be computed by repeated squaring using at most $2\\lceil\\log_2(f+1)\\rceil$ integer multiplications, with at most $f\\lceil\\log_2p\\rceil+1$ magnitude bits. The input convention gives $a\\le c+m$, so these bounds depend only on crossings, components, and field size. The coloring count is invariant under ambient isotopy of the oriented link. Field operations count as unit-cost operations; no bit-time bound for those operations or polynomial-time evaluation claim is made.",
