          "statement": "Assume countable choice. Let $K:S^1\\to S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot with a supplied product tubular embedding $\\Phi:S^1\\times D^2_{2\\epsilon}\\to S^3$. There is a polygonal knot $P$ inside this tube and a smooth rounding $K'$ of $P$ such that $K'$ is smoothly ambient isotopic to $K$ by an isotopy supported in the tube. If $K$ is supplied with a finite regular projection diagram $D$, the polygonal approximation and rounding can be chosen to have the same crossing code as $D$. A homeomorphism supported in the larger tube carries $P$ to $K'$, preserves the boundary torus of the radius-$\\epsilon$ tube, and sends each marked fibre meridian to itself up to orientation. Conversely, every polygonal knot $P$ with a finite product regular neighborhood can be rounded in pairwise disjoint vertex balls to a smooth knot $P^\\sharp$; a homeomorphism supported in those balls carries its compact exterior and marked meridian to those of $P^\\sharp$. If a regular projection diagram is supplied, the rounding balls can be chosen away from crossing charts, so the crossing code is unchanged. These clauses compare smooth knot type through smooth knots and polygonal exterior models through homeomorphisms; they do not treat a nonsmooth polygonal curve as a smooth isotopy slice.",
