          "statement": "For every valid connected one-component oriented planar knot code with $n$ crossings together with a specified polygonal realization $P$ whose regular projection has that code, there is an explicit finite combinatorial construction of a triangulated $S^3$ containing that same $P$ as a subcomplex. An abstract valid code can first be realized by the stated reconstruction procedure and then used as $P$. A **good exterior triangulation** is the triangulation inherited on the complement of the interior of a regular neighborhood made from one fixed ball around each vertex of $P$ and one fixed tube block along each edge, with its boundary torus triangulated and a meridian path marked. The construction gives a good exterior triangulation with at most $C(n+1)$ tetrahedra for one universal constant $C$, and the marked meridian is a path of at most $C(n+1)$ boundary edges. At $n=0$ the construction uses a fixed solid-torus exterior triangulation.\n\nA local crossing is lifted by separating the over- and under-strands in the interval direction; the schematic below records only the projected crossing, with the continuous strand passing over the gapped strand.\n\n~~~tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.15]\n\\draw[very thick] (-1,-1)--(1,1);\n\\draw[thick] (-1,1)--(-0.18,0.18);\n\\draw[thick] (0.18,-0.18)--(1,-1);\n\\node at (0.62,0.78) {over};\n\\node at (-0.70,0.76) {under};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n~~~\n\nThe picture records only the planar local incidence. The proof constructs disjoint arcs in a three-dimensional crossing block and does not infer embeddedness from the drawing.",
